    | Q&A            |\n| ----------------------- | -------- | -------------- |\n| A lively public webinar | Everyone | **Public Q&A** |"],[1134,"| Your situation                                         | First email             | Second email | Last email                                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------- | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Recorded webinar, where people watch on different days | Right after the session | A day later  | Just before a rolling deadline, written as a number of days |"],[1131,"| Your situation                              | First email             | Second email | Last email                  |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------ | --------------------------- |\n| Offer or replay closes in five days or more | Right after the session | A day later  | The day before the deadline |"],[1133,"| Your situation                         | First email             | Second email                                     | Last email  |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ----------- |\n| No deadline, such as customer training | Right after the session | A day later, if you have something useful to add | None needed |"],[1132,"| Your situation                         | First email             | Second email | Last email                  |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------ | --------------------------- |\n| Offer or replay closes within 48 hours | Right after the session | Skip it      | The morning of the deadline |"],[3485,"1. Add your presenters and moderators"],[150,"1. Describe your webinar"],[1306,"1. Open People"],[3793,"1. Open the Library"],[1453,"1. Open the tab and set the period"],[3619,"1. Open the webinar room"],[1288,"15 U.S. Code 7704, Legal Information Institute: the CAN-SPAM rules on opt-outs, postal addresses and subject lines, in full."],[3794,"2. Check your videos and where each one is hosted"],[3486,"2. Enter the green room"],[3620,"2. Pass the device check"],[1454,"2. Read the funnel"],[1307,"2. Read the table"],[152,"2. Review the script"],[1308,"3. Filter the list"],[3795,"3. Find recordings of webinars you've run"],[1455,"3. Find where people drop off"],[153,"3. Generate the pages"],[3621,"3. Get ready in the green room"],[3487,"3. Read the room"],[3796,"4. Add images for your pages"],[3488,"4. Bring people backstage and onto the stage"],[1456,"4. Check engagement and offers"],[3622,"4. Check recording"],[1309,"4. Combine filters for common follow-ups"],[154,"4. Generate the emails"],[1310,"5. Export the result"],[3489,"5. Finish your checks"],[155,"5. Generate the polls"],[3797,"5. Keep documents in Files"],[1457,"5. See the people behind the numbers"],[3623,"5. Style the room"],[3798,"6. Add new media"],[1458,"6. Check revenue"],[156,"6. Generate the offers"],[3490,"6. Leave the green room"],[3624,"6. Switch on streaming destinations (optional)"],[3625,"7. Go live"],[157,"7. Land on your finished webinar"],[3799,"7. Use a video or recording in a webinar"],[3626,"8. Present"],[3627,"9. End the session"],[5179,"A **Shorten URL's** toggle gives you shorter versions."],[73,"A **team member** has a login. A **presenter** or **moderator** runs a specific webinar and is added on that webinar's Overview tab. Presenters and moderators can join with a link, without an account."],[1142,"A 2011 Harvard Business Review article on online sales enquiries reported that most companies weren't responding nearly fast enough. It looked at companies handling online queries, not webinars, and it's an old study. Registering is still a similar first move, which is one more reason to send your first email today."],[4781,"A coach adds the **Phone** field so the final reminder can go by text (Beta), and turns on **GDPR Compliance** with a mandatory checkbox agreeing to follow-up emails."],[3023,"A coach holds free weekly office hours with **No end date**. Registrants see only the next session unless the coach changes **Schedule Preferences**."],[283,"A coach launching a new programme generates the whole webinar in one sitting, downloads the script as a PDF to rehearse from, and picks **Soft Pitch** because the audience is new to them."],[5382,"A coach or educator runs a live, ticketed workshop. The registration page sells a general admission ticket, the **Live + Replay bundle** sells the recording to people who can't attend live, and the confirmation page gives each buyer their join link and calendar buttons."],[3205,"A coach records a signature talk once and broadcasts it on a regular schedule, joining the chat when they can. Polls and offers work as they would live, so the session still ends with a next step."],[2186,"A coach running a live masterclass leaves chat on **Everyone** so attendees can introduce themselves and react, and uses **Private Q&A** to collect questions for the end. A moderator joins on the moderator link to keep up with the chat."],[1558,"A coach running paid workshops checks the **Revenue** view after each session, compares **Paid Registrations** with offer sales under **Revenue by Source**, and keeps an eye on **Checkout abandoned**."],[4059,"A coach runs a free masterclass and closes the replay on a **Specific date** that matches the deadline on their offer. The **Watched replay** event in their CRM tags people who caught up, so the follow-up matches what each person saw."],[3749,"A coach runs a live masterclass alone. They blur the background in the green room, keep recording on, launch a poll about the audience's situation halfway through, and push a booking offer near the end. **End of Session** sends everyone to the booking page."],[5563,"A coach runs a weekly live session. Before each one they open the Overview, check the **Upcoming** tab for the right date, glance at **Registrants**, and use **Enter Room** to get into the green room early."],[3578,"A coach runs an interview-style masterclass with a guest expert who has no EasyWebinar account. The guest joins by link, waits backstage during the coach's opening, and comes on stage for the interview segment."],[4352,"A coach sets the **Sender name** to their own name and the **Reply-to email** to the inbox they check every day. Registrants often reply to the confirmation with a question about the session, and that reply becomes the first real conversation with a prospect."],[2350,"A coach uses Create with AI with the goals \"understanding pain points\" and \"qualifying leads\", then edits the five generated polls into their own words. The polls run through the session and set up the call offer at the close."],[4910,"A coach's website has a \"Free masterclass\" page. The registration widget sits on that page, under the coach's own story, so the page they already designed does the persuading."],[3189,"A common approach is for a teacher to give the taster live every time, which can mean twelve sittings a term. Teachers may end up repeating the same session and fielding the same questions. In a busy week, the session can come out rushed or uneven."],[2614,"A common approach is for the instructor to read out a checkout link near the end and paste it into chat. In practice, that link tends to scroll away within a minute, and people who joined late may never see it. The company may also struggle to tell whether weak sales came from the bundle or from the checkout."],[2295,"A common approach is to ask questions out loud and read the replies as they scroll past in the chat. A few confident attendees answer while most stay silent. Afterwards, the partners scroll back through the chat log, trying to remember who said what. The call list ends up built on guesses."],[3373,"A common approach is to keep presenting it live, or to upload the recording and create a new dated session each week. Presenting live costs an engineer's hour every Tuesday. The dated route relies on someone remembering the setup. Either way, a prospect who signs up on a Wednesday waits six days to see the product."],[2135,"A common approach is to leave a public chat open and hope people keep personal details out of it. In practice, attendees tend to hold back on money questions when strangers can read them. The chat fills with greetings while the real questions stay unasked, and interest may cool before anyone follows up."],[1819,"A common approach is to leave the chat on and hope viewers start talking. In practice, people who arrive alone tend to stay quiet, so each one sees an empty panel. Questions about reading pace and meeting times may go unasked, and some readers leave unsure whether the club suits them."],[1508,"A common approach is to rebuild the funnel from exports. Sign-ups come from the form tool, attendance from the meeting tool and bookings from the sales calendar, and someone lines them up in a spreadsheet. That can take days each month. Names may not match across three lists, and a person who watched live and then on the recording can be counted twice."],[2829,"A common approach is to rebuild the webinar every month, or to keep reusing one webinar and clear it out. Rebuilding can eat an evening of copying pages, emails and the offer by hand. Reusing one webinar tends to mix last month's sign-ups into this month's numbers."],[1981,"A common approach is to record the deadline into the pitch, or to add a third-party timer to the sales page the offer links to. A recorded line such as \"the bonus ends on Friday\" is true for one week and wrong for every viewer after it. A timer set to one date can't follow sessions that start at different times. The coach ends up correcting timers, and some viewers are shown a deadline that has already passed."],[4319,"A common approach is to rely on a default email sent from a generic address. It may land in a promotions folder, from a name the buyer doesn't know. Replies may go to an inbox nobody reads. The broker may not even see who signed up until the day."],[4605,"A common approach is to run the explainer at set times, two or three a week. HR managers tend to read newsletters between interviews and payroll deadlines, though. A Thursday afternoon slot may not fit, and some may decide the topic can wait. For them, the newsletter click is probably wasted."],[4458,"A common approach is to schedule reminder emails by hand for each session date. That may work for one session a month, but it gets hard with several a week. Gaps creep in, and some people may hear nothing between signing up and the start."],[5336,"A common approach is to sell tickets on a separate payment page, then add each buyer to the webinar's attendee list manually. The day before, someone checks the payment list against the sign-up list. A buyer missed in the copying never gets a join link, and the consultant finds out only when that founder emails at the start time."],[3534,"A common approach is to send every speaker the same link and start at the advertised time. In practice, the first minutes tend to go on sound checks and guests asking whether they can be heard. Those minutes also open the recording that clients watch later."],[3010,"A common approach is to set up a separate event for every evening, which can mean sixteen events per course. Each one may need its own sign-up link, so trainees often juggle a list of links. When a holiday moves a class, someone usually has to rebuild that night by hand."],[90,"A common approach is to share one login and keep every client's webinars in the same account. That tends to hold until two people need the room at once, or someone edits the wrong firm's webinar. Billing sits one click away from everyone, and nobody can say who changed what. The cost is usually small at first, then grows with every new client."],[2456,"A common approach is to take questions in the main chat and read a few out at the end. In a busy room, those questions sit between greetings and reactions, so the presenter scrolls back and still misses some. Questions about one customer's account are also on show to every other customer. Admins whose questions were missed often raise a support ticket the next day."],[4877,"A common route is a \"Register here\" button on the treatment page that sends visitors to a separate webinar page. That works. But each click away is a chance to lose a reader, and the page that persuaded them gets left behind. The cost probably shows up as fewer signups than the page's traffic suggests."],[4735,"A common route is to collect the extra details in a separate form tool, then match those answers to the webinar's registrant list by hand. That can work for a small list. But the matching probably eats an afternoon each quarter, and consent records can end up split across two systems."],[5016,"A common route is to design the page in a separate landing page tool, then connect its form to the webinar platform. Each month, someone retypes the new date on the page. That can work. But each handoff is a place where a form breaks or an old date slips out. The cost likely shows up only as fewer signups."],[918,"A common route is to stitch the launch together from a meeting app, an email tool and a video host. For an automated version, the recording moves to the video host, and the sign-up page, reminders and offer are rebuilt around it. The live and automated versions then drift apart, and each keeps its own list of sign-ups."],[1125,"A common route is to write one email, paste in the replay link and send it to the whole list. That's quick, but it tends to read wrong for someone. People who watched get a recording they may not need, and people who missed it get thanked for coming. Splitting the list by hand can mean exporting names from one tool and pasting them into another."],[1666,"A common setup is one fixed showing each evening, so the team can promote a single time. That probably suits people who plan ahead. Shoppers who click an ad at lunchtime, though, may face a wait of several hours. Many are likely to forget, and the ad spend that brought them is partly wasted."],[5553,"A common way to manage this is a shared spreadsheet and a long email thread. The date sits in one place, the speaker's link in another and sign-up numbers in a third. It tends to work until a busy month. Then a guest may get the wrong link, or a called-off date may still be promoted."],[208,"A common workaround is to build each piece in a different tool: slides in one, a landing page in another, emails from a template. That can take several evenings. The pieces may drift apart as they're written, and a first webinar can slip while the setup drags on."],[706,"A common workaround is to download the attendee list after each session, clean it up and import it into HubSpot. That tends to take a day or two, and the file usually holds only names and whether people attended. Reps then call everyone in the same order, and the warmest leads may cool while they wait."],[1357,"A common workaround is to download the full registrant list after each session and split it between agents alphabetically. Every name then gets the same call, in the same order. Warm prospects may wait behind people who never turned up, and nobody can say which channel produced the viewers."],[5189,"A common workaround is to send the guest a shared login, or a link that drops them straight in front of the audience. That can mean passwords passed around and a guest testing their microphone in public. Long registration URLs in show notes are also awkward to read aloud or retype."],[4012,"A common workaround is to upload the recording to a video site and email the link the next morning. That probably reaches some of the people who missed the evening. Yet the offer and the polls tend to stay behind in the live room, so the recording teaches without selling. Viewers may also keep watching for months, long after enrolment has closed."],[4342,"A confirmation only goes out when someone registers, and your registration link doesn't work until the registration page is published. Saving the page alone isn't enough. Open the **Registration Page** from the **Content** tab and click **Publish** before you promote the link. See Registration page builder."],[4052,"A connected CRM can act on replay behaviour with the **Watched replay** and **Didn't watch replay** events, and receive each contact's **Webinar Replay Link** or **Webinar Replay Short Link** in a custom field. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[2681,"A connected CRM can act on the **Clicked offer** event, which fires when someone clicks your offer. A sensible mapping is a `webinar-hot` tag plus a notification to sales."],[5212,"A connected CRM can receive each attendee's personal join link and replay link through the **Custom fields** tab. Mapping the join link is what lets you send reminders from your CRM instead of EasyWebinar, with each contact getting their own working link."],[1402,"A connected CRM can receive the same behaviour as tags: **Missed**, **Watched full**, **Clicked offer** and more. People complements that by letting you look back across webinars and export a segment on demand. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[1551,"A connected CRM can tag people by the same behaviour Analytics counts: **Registered**, **Attended**, **Missed**, **Watched replay**, **Watched full** and **Clicked offer**, among others. That lets your sales or email system act on what the numbers show. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[4348,"A connected CRM or email tool can act on the same moment through the **Registered** event, for example tagging every new registrant. On the **Custom fields** side, the **Webinar Join Link** can be mapped into a CRM field so each contact holds their own working link. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[5378,"A connected CRM or email tool can tag paid registrants as they sign up (the **Registered** event), and tag what they did in the session, from **Attended** and **Missed** to **Watched full** and **Clicked offer**. Map the **Webinar Join Link** into a CRM field if you want to send reminders from your own system. See Connect a CRM and map your webinar data."],[4765,"A connected CRM or email tool has a **Custom fields** tab that sends webinar details into your CRM's own fields. The data it lists is:"],[3022,"A consultant charges for a live masterclass by choosing **Paid** on the pricing step. Buyers complete a Stripe checkout before their registration goes through."],[4635,"A consultant puts an explainer webinar on demand for prospects to watch before a first call, so the call can start from where the webinar left off."],[1651,"A conventional webinar session sits at a time you chose in advance. A just-in-time session is generated around the person registering: EasyWebinar offers them a session that begins shortly, on a cycle set by the **Interval**."],[2808,"A copy that keeps the original's type is ready to edit straight away. A copy that moves from live to simulive or evergreen needs one more thing: a recording to play."],[2029,"A countdown persuades because people trust it. Keep that trust:"],[1970,"A countdown timer is a visible clock that shows the time remaining before something happens. In EasyWebinar it does one of two jobs:"],[1974,"A countdown turns a deadline you would otherwise keep repeating (\"the bonus closes when the session ends\") into something every attendee can see. On a registration page, it makes the session feel like an event with a start time, not a page to come back to later."],[4779,"A course creator adds a custom field asking for the student's current level, so the pitch at the end can speak to beginners and advanced students differently. **UTM Params** shows which posts and emails filled the session."],[5566,"A course creator runs an evergreen webinar every day. The Overview shows the generated run of upcoming sessions, and the **Live** tab lets them enter a session while it is running."],[5220,"A course creator sends no-shows the replay link in a **Webinar Follow-up** after the session, and maps the join link into their email tool so reminders come from their own system."],[5075,"A course creator starts from an **Education** template and saves their styled version to **My Templates**, so every launch looks consistent. They add a field asking for the student's current level and turn on UTM capture to see which posts drive signups."],[4355,"A course creator uses **Create with AI** to draft the sequence, then rewrites the welcome email in their own voice and sets the sender to a name their students already know. See selling online courses with webinars."],[883,"A course launch, end to end"],[4211,"A creator already has a teaching video on YouTube. They import it with **Import from URL**, enter its exact duration, and use no storage. Later they replace it with a recording of a live session through the **Webinar Video** panel. See selling online courses with webinars."],[5383,"A creator charges a small fee for a live training that previews a full course, then presents the course itself as an offer inside the session. Registration revenue and course sales appear separately under **Revenue by Source**. See selling online courses with webinars."],[282,"A creator describes a training webinar that teaches the core idea of a course, chooses the online course category and **Balanced Approach** for the offer, and edits the script before generating pages. See Course creators."],[1406,"A creator filters for high watch time plus **Offer clicked** after each evergreen session and exports the list for a personal follow-up, since those viewers were interested enough to click but didn't enrol. See Course creators."],[4634,"A creator offers a free training session on demand, so new subscribers can start it the moment they sign up. On a separate sales webinar, the creator leaves On Demand off so the session feels like an event people turn up for. This is one pattern in selling online courses with webinars."],[2349,"A creator opens with a poll about the audience's experience level, then asks about their biggest obstacle halfway through. Just before the enrolment offer, a poll about timing shows how many people are ready to start now."],[3902,"A creator runs a launch webinar live with recording on. Afterwards, the session sits in **Recordings**, and the creator clones the webinar as evergreen and picks that recording, so the version that sold well live keeps running. See selling online courses with webinars."],[2841,"A creator runs a teaching webinar live a few times with recording on, then clones it as **Evergreen**, selects the recording of the strongest session on the copy, and sets an evergreen schedule. The live original stays as it was. See Selling online courses with webinars."],[3386,"A creator runs a teaching webinar that ends with an enrolment offer, on a daily schedule with just-in-time sessions. The webinar sells the course while the course itself stays on the creator's own platform. See Selling online courses with webinars."],[3752,"A creator runs a training live a few times with recording on, then clones the strongest session as an evergreen webinar using the recording from the Library. The course creator's guide follows that route end to end."],[1557,"A creator selling a course through an evergreen webinar watches **Offers Performance** and **Clicks to Purchases**. A healthy click rate with few purchases sends them to the checkout page, not the presentation. See Course creators."],[718,"A creator selling a course through webinars tags **Clicked offer** and keeps those contacts out of the general nurture sequence. That stops buyers who are close to purchase from getting beginner emails."],[2498,"A creator teaching a free workshop uses **Public Q&A**. Attendees see that someone has already asked about prerequisites, and the creator answers the most common questions in a block before the enrolment offer."],[1880,"A creator turns their best live session into an evergreen webinar and writes a manual script from the questions people really asked live, with `Admin` replies that match the answers they gave. Every session plays the same tested conversation."],[4497,"A creator's evergreen webinar runs daily. Because reminder timing is relative to the webinar, the same three emails serve the whole schedule. The creator adds a Beta **Reminder SMS** as the final nudge for an audience that reads texts faster than email. See selling online courses with webinars."],[1710,"A creator's teaching webinar runs on just-in-time alongside a daily recurring time. New visitors from ads take the next just-in-time session; people who'd rather plan ahead book the daily slot. This pattern is part of selling online courses with webinars."],[3577,"A customer success manager presents a customer training session while a colleague joins with the **Moderator Join Link for Live room** to handle questions. The moderator never needs to appear on stage, so they stay off it for the whole session."],[104,"A customer success team adds a colleague who handles questions during onboarding sessions. On a one-off session, the **Moderator Join Link for Live room** does the same job without a login."],[3905,"A customer success team keeps its product FAQ and onboarding guides on the **Files** tab as the AI Moderator's knowledge base (from Pro onward), so questions on an unattended evergreen onboarding webinar can be answered from the team's own documents."],[4058,"A customer success team records an onboarding session and leaves the replay on **Always Available**, so new customers can watch it whenever they join. The **Replay Page Link** goes into the team's welcome material."],[5219,"A customer success team runs a training webinar for existing customers. It emails the **One-Click Registration Link** to the customer list, then sends the **Attendee Replay link** in a follow-up to everyone who couldn't attend live."],[4632,"A customer success team runs its product onboarding as an evergreen webinar with On Demand on. New customers register and start the walkthrough immediately, and the button reads *Start onboarding* instead of the default."],[4498,"A customer success team runs onboarding webinars for new customers. Their reminders restate what to have ready before the session, and the fifteen-minute reminder carries the link and nothing else."],[1407,"A customer success team runs recurring onboarding webinars and filters **Behavior** for no-shows on the latest session, then sends that group the replay so new customers don't fall behind."],[1712,"A customer success team sends new customers to an onboarding webinar with just-in-time sessions, so each customer starts onboarding in their own timezone shortly after signing up."],[2844,"A customer success team that presents onboarding live clones the webinar as **Simulive** and chooses the live recording, so the session can run on a schedule while the team covers the chat."],[3204,"A customer success team turns its onboarding talk into a recurring simulive session. New customers register for a date, and a team member covers the chat to pick up account-specific questions."],[3388,"A customer success team turns its onboarding walkthrough into an evergreen webinar with On Demand switched on, so new customers can watch the moment they sign up instead of waiting for a scheduled session."],[1701,"A daily or weekly **Recurring Schedule** can run alongside just-in-time, for registrants who prefer to book a set time. Those sessions use the webinar's timezone."],[4626,"A daily or weekly schedule can run alongside On Demand for registrants who want to book a time. Recurring sessions run in the webinar's timezone."],[4027,"A deadline creates urgency, which is why most offers sit behind one. If your offers or follow-up emails mention when the replay closes, make sure they match the option you chose here."],[1556,"A demand generation team runs a monthly webinar and tracks **Visitors** to **Registered** after every promotion change. When the rate drops, they test a new registration page headline before changing anything else."],[2481,"A few habits that keep the queue under control:"],[470,"A few things save time once you start. If you're verifying a domain, you need access to its DNS settings, or a colleague who has it. That colleague can work from their own team member login. If you're connecting a provider, have your account credentials to hand. You also need a reply address that a person actually reads."],[802,"A full build takes about 10 to 15 minutes."],[928,"A good prompt says who the webinar is for, the three or four things you'll teach, how long it runs, the tone you want, and what you're selling at the end. A vague prompt produces a generic webinar."],[5199,"A link for an existing list. It carries the person's name and email in the URL, so a known contact registers without filling in the form. Because it depends on details you already hold, it only works for contacts you already have. Use it in emails to your own list, where you know who each recipient is."],[2687,"A live masterclass ends with a free offer whose button opens a booking page for a call. **Attendee Action Follow-ups** reach people who clicked but didn't book."],[2026,"A live masterclass ends with an offer to book a call. The countdown shows how long the booking link stays open after the session, and the registration-page countdown builds attendance in the days before."],[2497,"A live product demo runs **Private Q&A**, so prospects can ask about pricing or their own setup without the room seeing. A solutions engineer joins as moderator and works the **Active** list while the presenter demos."],[4207,"A live session recorded with **Auto Recording** on lands in **Library → Recordings**, already hosted on EasyWebinar. To run it as an automated webinar, clone the live webinar, choose **Evergreen** or **Simulive** for the copy, and pick the recording. See Clone a webinar and Run a live webinar."],[1806,"A live webinar has a shared room: everyone is there at the same time, so the chat fills itself. An evergreen webinar doesn't. Each registrant picks their own session, attendees arrive at different times, and a genuinely shared chat isn't possible. Without anything in it, the chat panel looks abandoned."],[3001,"A live webinar is a session you present in real time. Attendees join at the scheduled moment, and chat and questions happen as they happen. Live is one of three webinar types; simulive and evergreen play a recording instead. The schedule you set here decides when registrants can attend, and the registration page is where they sign up for it."],[2971,"A live webinar runs at one fixed time for everyone, in the timezone you set. Your registration page shows that time with its timezone. Choose the timezone you will broadcast in."],[3002,"A live webinar suits sessions where your presence is the point: a launch, a workshop or a class with real questions. Creating it fixes the date, time and timezone that people sign up for."],[488,"A low open rate usually points to the subject line or the sender, so recheck your sending identity along with your wording. A good open rate with a low click rate means the email was opened and the offer didn't land, which isn't a setup problem."],[666,"A mapping is three choices: **When** (the event) → **Then** (the action) → **Value** (the tag). Pick each one and click **Add mapping**."],[4499,"A marketing team already runs its nurture emails from its own system, so it maps the **Webinar Join Link** into a CRM field and sends one of the reminders from there, keeping EasyWebinar's reminders for the final hour."],[2842,"A marketing team clones last quarter's webinar for a new topic. The registration page layout, reminder emails and integration settings come across, and the team rewrites the content rather than rebuilding the structure."],[102,"A marketing team gives **Admin** to the person who owns the plan and settings. Colleagues who build registration pages and follow up on registrants work as **User**."],[717,"A marketing team maps **Missed** and **Didn't watch replay** to tags that start a replay sequence. People who still haven't watched get a second nudge from the email tool the team already uses."],[3021,"A marketing team presents a quarterly product briefing at one fixed time. It offers a second sitting with **Add Session** for prospects in other regions."],[5218,"A marketing team promotes the **Registration Page Link** in ads and social posts with **Shorten URL's** on. For an email to existing customers, it uses the **One-Click Registration Link**, so customers register without retyping details the team already holds."],[3202,"A marketing team records its strongest product presentation and runs it as a weekly simulive session. A product specialist joins the chat for each sitting and answers questions while the recording plays, so prospects get a consistent pitch and real answers."],[2352,"A marketing team running a recurring educational webinar ends with a feedback poll on delivery, so each run tells the presenter whether the teaching is landing before the next session is planned."],[4353,"A marketing team running a webinar for pipeline turns on **Notify admin** and points it at a team address, so sales can see registrations as they arrive. In the connected CRM, the **Registered** event tags each new contact for the campaign."],[1405,"A marketing team running paid campaigns turns on **UTM Params** for every webinar, then filters People by **Source & UTM** each month to compare which channels produce attendees with high **Watch percentage**, not just sign-ups."],[4057,"A marketing team runs a live panel, then uses **Custom Build** to give the replay its own layout and a call to action aimed at people who weren't in the room. **Replay Watched** in Analytics shows how much of the audience came through the replay."],[3751,"A marketing team runs a panel webinar with branded frame overlays and streams it to the company's LinkedIn page through EasyCast. Registered attendees watch in the webinar room, and the overlay carries the brand into the recording too."],[3576,"A marketing team runs a panel with two guest speakers from partner companies. Each guest gets the **Co-presenter Join Link**, joins fifteen minutes early, and checks their audio backstage. The host introduces the topic alone, then moves each guest onto the stage in turn."],[5565,"A marketing team runs a webinar for a product launch. The Overview gives them the **Registration Page Link** to promote, and the gap between **Registrants** and **Live Attended** tells them, after the session, whether reminders did their job."],[4780,"A marketing team runs paid ads to a webinar. It turns on **UTM Params**, tags each ad, and filters **People** by **Source & UTM** after the session to compare campaigns by who actually attended. The phone number is mapped into the CRM so sales has it."],[518,"A marketing team sending regularly gets the most from **Domain Configuration**, because volume is where a verified domain pays back. If the company already runs SES, SendGrid or Mailgun, connect that account with **Add Service** and agree who looks after the credentials."],[4908,"A marketing team writes a blog post on the webinar's topic and embeds the registration form at the end, so readers who reach the bottom can sign up on the spot. The **Registration Page Link** goes into the newsletter and paid social."],[2499,"A monthly onboarding webinar runs as a simulive session. Q&A stays private, and the team answers setup questions in real time while the recording plays, then checks the **Not Answered Rate** afterwards."],[5594,"A new webinar shows zeros across the board until registrations start."],[5310,"A paid webinar asks people to pay before they are registered. In EasyWebinar you choose **Paid** when you create the webinar, set a price, and attendees pay through a Stripe checkout on their way to registering. The money goes straight to your own Stripe account, and every payment appears in the webinar's Overview and Analytics. Paid registration launched in **March 2026**."],[4046,"A paid webinar can sell a **Live + Replay bundle**, a second option that includes the replay recording with its own price. If you sell replay access, set the replay's **Availability** to match what the bundle promises. See Paid webinars."],[5375,"A paid webinar can still sell. Add offers on the **CTAs** tab, with an optional price and countdown timer, and offer sales are reported under **Paid Offer**, separately from registration revenue. Buyers of an offer receive the **Paid Offer — Transaction Success** email."],[2500,"A panel discussion is tightly scripted, so Q&A is set to **Disable** and chat to **Hosts only**. Questions still reach the team privately, without interrupting the panel."],[2711,"A poll asks a question. An offer sells something, with a headline, a button and an optional price."],[2282,"A poll has three parts:"],[2271,"A poll in EasyWebinar is a question with a set of answers that you put in front of your audience during a webinar. You build polls on the webinar's **CTAs** tab, next to your offers, and show them in the room when you choose. During a live session, each poll goes out when you launch it from the room's **Polls** panel. Create with AI can also write a set of polls for you, built around what you want the polls to achieve."],[2287,"A poll puts one question to the whole room, with a fixed set of answers. Everyone replies in the same format, so the results are easy to read at a glance. It also gives attendees something to do besides watch."],[3543,"A presenter or moderator joining by link doesn't need an EasyWebinar account. Treat both links as private: anyone holding one can join your room in that role. The registration, attendee and replay links are the ones meant for your audience."],[2351,"A product demo asks attendees which problem brought them, then shows the part of the product that solves it. After the session, the team filters **People** by webinar and looks at who answered polls and clicked the trial offer."],[3750,"A product manager presents a release walkthrough or product demo while a colleague moderates. The colleague waits backstage, handles the **Question** queue during the demo, and comes on stage for the last ten minutes to answer the top questions. **Share** carries the product demo, and the layout favours the screen."],[1709,"A product team links its \"watch the demo\" button to an evergreen registration page with just-in-time on a short interval. A visitor registers and is watching the recorded demo within minutes, in their own local time."],[4633,"A product team offers a recorded feature walkthrough with On Demand alongside just-in-time. Evaluators in a hurry watch straight away; others join a session a few minutes later."],[3387,"A product team puts a recorded demo behind its \"see it in action\" link. With just-in-time on, a visitor who registers can be watching within minutes, while the details are still fresh in their mind."],[4210,"A product team records a demo for an evergreen webinar and uploads it with **Local import**. The demo isn't published anywhere else, and hosting it on EasyWebinar keeps it private to the webinar."],[3579,"A product team rehearses a product launch demo in the green room: they test the screen share of the product, pick a layout that gives the demo most of the stage, and switch on a LinkedIn Organisation destination in EasyCast before going live."],[1559,"A product team running onboarding webinars cares most about **Total Attended** and **Replay only**, because many users catch up on the recording. A high **Not Answered Rate** tells them to go back through the questions and cover the gaps in their follow-up."],[4909,"A product team runs a monthly product webinar and keeps the widget in the sidebar of its help centre or product blog, so customers looking for help can register for the next session without leaving."],[5077,"A product team runs a recurring demo webinar. It lets **Create with AI** draft the registration page from the demo script, edits the headline and bio, and checks the page on mobile before publishing."],[4496,"A product team runs a weekly live demo. The three-reminder pattern goes out before every session without anyone touching it, and the team watches **Registered** to attended week by week to see whether a subject-line change made a difference."],[1882,"A product team runs an on-demand demo. A manual script places a short exchange about integrations at the point in the video where integrations come up, so the answer appears just as viewers are wondering."],[3203,"A product team serving several regions schedules the same recorded walkthrough at times that suit each one. Each session runs in the webinar's timezone, so the team sets times for each region accordingly."],[285,"A product team sets **Type** to **Evergreen** for an always-on product walkthrough, chooses poll goals for qualifying leads and segmenting for follow-up, then attaches their recorded demo as the video."],[3903,"A product team uploads its demo with **Local import** rather than publishing it on YouTube, so the video stays private to the webinar. The **EasyWebinar** badge confirms where it is hosted, and the storage meter shows the space it takes."],[1054,"A quick first message"],[1433,"A rating of how engaged someone was, based on attendance, watch time and interaction."],[2688,"A recorded demo carries an offer for a trial, with the button pointing at the sign-up page. The team watches **Views to Clicks** across sessions to judge whether the demo builds enough interest."],[2028,"A recorded product demo ends with an offer for a trial or a call with sales. A countdown is optional here; many teams use it only during launch periods."],[5384,"A recorded training is sold on an evergreen schedule, so people pay and watch at a session time that suits them. The same pricing step applies, and every purchase lands in **Analytics → Revenue**."],[4194,"A recorded webinar has a **Webinar Video** panel on its **Content** tab that live webinars don't. It shows the video you chose and its length."],[1694,"A recorded webinar has no live room for you to enter, so you check it by joining a running session. The quickest way to get one is to register yourself."],[5537,"A recorded webinar has no live room to enter. On an evergreen webinar, wait for a session to be running: the **Sessions** panel's **Live** tab shows it, marked *Live now*, with **Enter room**."],[5596,"A recorded webinar has no live room to enter. When a session is running, it appears on the **Live** tab of the **Sessions** panel with **Enter room**."],[3348,"A recording of a webinar you have already run sits under **Recordings**. Choosing it turns that session into the content of the evergreen webinar."],[3439,"A recording plays at times you set, like a scheduled broadcast. Everyone in a session sees the same moment, so the chat still works as a shared room, and you can answer questions live while the recording plays."],[3440,"A recording runs on a repeating schedule, and each registrant picks a time that suits them. Use it when the webinar is part of a funnel that should work whether or not you are available."],[4316,"A registrant without a confirmation has only what they saw on the thank-you page. If they closed it, they may have nothing to come back to. The first email they see may then arrive days later, from a name they don't connect with signing up."],[4447,"A reminder is an email in the **Webinar Reminders** group on the **Notifications** tab. It goes to people who registered, before the webinar starts, and its purpose is to turn a registration into an attendance."],[4004,"A replay gives everyone who registered a second chance at the session. It serves people who couldn't make the live time, people who left early and people who want to watch again. For a webinar that ends in an offer, it also extends the window to act on the offer."],[4656,"A replay is the recording of a past session, on the **Replay** tab. Instant replay, or On Demand, lets someone watch an evergreen webinar straight away without waiting for a session."],[4085,"A replay is the recording of a past session. Instant replay, or **On Demand**, is an evergreen option that lets someone watch straight away without waiting for a session."],[3986,"A replay page is where people watch the recording of a webinar session after it has happened. In EasyWebinar every webinar has a **Replay** tab that turns the page on, gives you its link, sets how long the replay stays available, decides where viewers go when the recording finishes, and chooses whether the page copies the live webinar or is designed separately. Most people who register don't make the live session, so the replay is often how the webinar reaches the rest of its audience."],[2184,"A replay page set to **Webinar Replica** shows the same video, offers, polls and chat replay as the session."],[716,"A RevOps team maps **Clicked offer** and **Watched full** to separate tags, so lead routing in the CRM can treat them as different signals. The **Webinar Title** field records which webinar each contact came from."],[872,"A room that doesn't feel empty"],[103,"A sales team running demos gives a login to each sales engineer who presents every week. A partner presenting once joins with the Co-presenter link instead."],[2187,"A sales-led demo uses **Hosts only**, so prospects from different companies can ask about pricing or their setup without the rest of the room seeing. Each conversation stays between that attendee and the team."],[1050,"A sender people recognise"],[339,"A sending domain isn't a page domain"],[4406,"A sensible schedule"],[1733,"A session that starts a few minutes after someone registers, instead of at a fixed future time. In EasyWebinar it's an option on evergreen webinars."],[1860,"A short script looks like this:"],[1084,"A short text nudge (Beta)"],[4596,"A simple rule: if you want people to watch the moment they register, use On Demand on an evergreen webinar. If you want people who missed a session to catch up, use the replay page."],[3197,"A simulive session can have a replay page for people who missed it, set on the **Replay** tab. See Replay pages."],[3108,"A simulive webinar plays a recording at the dates and times you schedule. Everyone in a session reaches the same point of the video at the same moment, so the chat behaves like one shared room, and you or your moderators can answer questions while the recording runs. Creating one takes five steps in the manual builder: name and type, schedule, pricing, video selection and registration template."],[2164,"A simulive webinar plays a recording at times you set, and everyone in a session sees the same moment of the video. That makes a shared chat possible. Set chat up on the **Engagement** tab as you would for a live webinar, and you and your moderators answer in real time while the recording plays. Being in the chat is most of what simulive gives you over evergreen."],[1711,"A solo consultant can't keep a calendar of live sessions. A just-in-time evergreen webinar means an enquiry from the website can be watching the consultant's explainer within minutes, with **Exclude specific time** keeping chosen hours clear."],[4213,"A solo consultant without a video platform account records a training, uploads it with **Local import**, and runs it as a simulive webinar. Nothing outside EasyWebinar is needed."],[5078,"A solo operator picks **Default Register Page**, changes the headline and background, and publishes. Every page has a working default, so the thank-you and replay pages can wait."],[3389,"A solo operator who can't present every day records a single webinar and runs it on a weekly schedule, with blackout dates over holidays and Chat Simulation keeping the room from looking empty."],[1881,"A solo operator without time to script uses AI Simulation, builds a cast with a mix of beginners, a sceptic and quieter observers, and reviews the cast before turning the simulation on."],[284,"A solo operator without time to write six emails keeps the default chips (script, pages and emails), leaves **Polls** and **Offers** off, and builds a single offer by hand on the **CTAs** tab."],[602,"A starting set of tags"],[1051,"A subject line that says what's inside"],[3604,"A team member has their own login to your EasyWebinar account. A presenter or moderator is running a specific webinar, and may be a guest with only a join link."],[77,"A team member is a person with their own login on your EasyWebinar account. Their role sets how much of the account they can change. **Admin** reaches every setting, billing included, while **User** gets basic access to features. Access is set by role alone, with no per-permission matrix."],[3571,"A team member is someone with their own EasyWebinar login, created under **Settings → Team** as an **Admin** or **User**. A presenter or moderator is someone running a specific webinar. Add a person to your team when they work in EasyWebinar regularly; send a join link when they're a guest on one session. Sharing one login between hosts means only one of you can be in the live room as a host. See Team members."],[5529,"A team member is someone with their own login to your EasyWebinar account, added under **Settings → Team** as an **Admin** or a **User**. A presenter or moderator is someone running a specific webinar, added on that webinar's Overview tab. Add someone to the team when they work in EasyWebinar regularly; send a join link when they're a guest on one session."],[719,"A team running one webinar per product line connects each webinar to its own list. When they launch a new live session from an existing one, cloning with **Integration settings** ticked keeps the mappings."],[3020,"A training provider runs the same class on set weekdays through a term. A **Weekly** schedule with an end date covers the term, and **Blackout Dates** cover the breaks."],[1844,"A useful check: look down the attendee list as a newcomer would meet that room. If almost everyone is an enthusiastic expert on high engagement, add curious and sceptical attendees, and move some of the most active ones to Medium or Low."],[3164,"A video on YouTube stays discoverable there unless you've unlisted it, which is a reason to upload a recording you'd rather keep to registrants."],[1222,"A webinar built with **Create with AI** already has **Follow-up — Instant** and **Follow-up — 1d** in its sequence, and **Add email** adds more. Those drafts arrive with merge tags in place, so dates, times and join links fill themselves in. Keep the tags and edit the words around them, then use the templates above to tighten the copy and split the audience."],[4333,"A webinar built with **Create with AI** arrives with its emails already written, starting with a welcome email sent on registration. Those AI drafts come with merge tags already in place, so dates, times and join links fill themselves in. Read the wording before you publish, and edit it like any other email."],[279,"A webinar built with AI has the same pricing options as one built by hand. If you're charging for registration, read the generated pages and emails carefully so they describe the price and what's included correctly. See Paid webinars."],[2683,"A webinar can charge for registration and still sell offers inside. Registration payments are reported under **Paid Webinar** and offer sales under **Paid Offer**."],[5327,"A webinar can do both: charge for entry and sell a follow-on offer inside. EasyWebinar reports the two separately under **Revenue by Source**."],[5181,"A webinar has several audiences at once. Some people still need to register, some are already registered, and some missed the session. Others are helping you run it. The Share dialog gives each group its own way in, from one screen."],[2624,"A webinar with no polls or offers yet shows **\"No CTAs found\"**."],[1347,"A webinar's own **Analytics** tab gives you counts: how many registered, how many attended, how many missed. **People** gives you the names behind those counts, for every webinar in your account at once."],[592,"A webinar's registration page collects registrants and can take payment through your own Stripe account. Registrants get reminder emails from the Notifications tab, and SMS or WhatsApp messages, both currently in Beta. They join the room live, or a recording plays at a scheduled time (simulive) or on demand (evergreen). Attendance, offer clicks and revenue flow into the webinar's Analytics and the People view, and out to connected CRMs and email tools, or into EasyCRM."],[3897,"A webinar's type can't be changed, so the route from live to automated is cloning. Clone the live webinar, choose **Evergreen** for the copy, and pick the live session's recording on the new webinar's **Content** tab. The recording is already in your library. See Clone a webinar."],[3746,"A webinar's type can't be changed, so the way to automate a successful live session is to clone it as **Evergreen** and pick its recording. See Clone a webinar."],[2979,"A weekly webinar runs until you stop it. Use **Blackout Dates** to skip individual days without ending the series, or set an end date to stop it."],[461,"A whole-list send would overrun the allowance, so the targeted list is the better first broadcast. Even if every address were at Gmail, the largest day stays well under the bulk level, though the rules for all senders still apply. Two complaints on 600 emails would pass 0.3%, so this rough guide favours people who expect to hear from you. Each provider measures its own traffic, so treat the last row as a planning figure."],[1226,"A workflow stays in Draft until you publish it, so you can paste in these templates and revise them before anything reaches a contact."],[5278,"Abandoned checkouts"],[306,"About 10 to 15 minutes end to end. The script is the slowest part, at roughly a minute and a half."],[576,"About this documentation"],[4087,"Add a **Webinar Follow-up** on the **Notifications** tab, set its audience to no-shows, and include the replay link."],[4527,"Add a **Webinar Follow-up** with its audience set to no-shows and include the replay link."],[1032,"Add a **Webinar Follow-up** with the audience set to no-shows and include the replay link, or tag them with the **Missed** event in your email tool."],[2551,"Add a deadline"],[4669,"Add a phone field, your own custom questions, a GDPR consent checkbox and UTM campaign capture to an EasyWebinar registration form, and see where that data shows up afterwards."],[6,"Add a team member"],[4108,"Add a video while you set up a webinar"],[4405,"Add another reminder"],[350,"Add call and SMS (Beta) sending"],[1079,"Add each follow-up"],[447,"Add each sending service to one SPF record, keeping a single record for the domain."],[4829,"Add it to your site"],[74,"Add someone to your team when they work in EasyWebinar regularly."],[4742,"Add the **Phone** field if you'll want to text people or keep their number."],[1850,"Add the names for **Attendees on Event Page**, separated by commas."],[762,"Add-ons"],[813,"Add-ons extend your plan without changing it. Switch one on with **Add to plan** on the **Add-ons** tab under **Settings → Manage Plan**."],[1038,"Add-ons: the **C-Name** and **White Label** add-ons mentioned on this page."],[2006,"Adjust its settings across the **Settings** and **Advanced** tabs."],[126,"Admin can change everything, including settings and billing. User gets basic access to the features."],[1534,"After a busy session, the **Not Answered Rate** on Q&A tells you how many questions you left behind. Those are worth answering in your follow-up email."],[4056,"After a live launch webinar, a creator sets the replay to **Expire after days**, keeps **Webinar Replica** so the enrolment offer stays on the page, and points **After the replay ends** at the course checkout. A follow-up to no-shows carries the replay link. See selling online courses with webinars."],[159,"After generating: finish the webinar"],[1130,"After that, your deadline sets the schedule. Count back from the moment the offer or the replay closes, not forward from the session."],[2183,"After the session, the webinar's **Analytics** tab has a **Chat** panel showing total messages, which you can filter by session."],[3016,"After the webinar exists, its **Overview** tab holds the schedule. The options menu on **Scheduling Options** changes the whole schedule, and the menu on a session row acts on one sitting. Click **Share** for the **Registration Page Link** you promote, or use the sharing links guide."],[2020,"After the webinar, open **Analytics**. **Offers Performance** shows views, clicks and purchases for each offer with a click rate. **Views to Clicks** and **Clicks to Purchases** show where the offer loses people, and **Revenue by Offer** shows what each offer earned. Comparing an offer with and without a countdown across sessions is the clearest way to see what the timer is doing for you."],[487,"After your first send, compare these cards with the industry averages shown beneath them. The figures here are sample data from a test account."],[27,"Agencies"],[361,"Agencies"],[1322,"Agencies"],[1778,"Agencies"],[2094,"Agencies"],[2568,"Agencies"],[2758,"Agencies"],[3818,"Agencies"],[4119,"Agencies"],[4276,"Agencies"],[4694,"Agencies"],[4841,"Agencies"],[4976,"Agencies"],[5147,"Agencies"],[5457,"Agencies"],[759,"AI credits"],[2087,"AI Moderation (from Pro onward)"],[2410,"AI Moderation (from Pro onward)"],[2174,"AI Moderation and simulated chat only work with **Easywebinar Chat**. If you want either of them, don't choose **Third party**."],[1618,"AI Moderator (from Pro onward)"],[3271,"AI Moderator (from Pro onward)"],[3814,"AI Moderator (from Pro onward)"],[1766,"AI or manual: which to use"],[287,"AI webinar-building features, from each platform's public documentation:"],[1284,"Aim for about 40 characters, with the useful words first, such as the replay or the deadline. That's Nielsen Norman Group's 2014 guidance, because inboxes can cut longer lines off."],[419,"Alignment is the idea that links all this to your goal. Your contacts see your From address, so these checks only help your name when they pass for that same domain."],[3449,"All three types use the same pages, emails, polls, offers and analytics. Only the delivery differs. Each webinar has these tabs:"],[545,"Almost always, the sending identity isn't verified or no provider is connected. Open **Configurations**, check the notice on **Email Integration**, and finish whichever step is missing. If only a workflow is silent, check that it's published, because a draft sends nothing."],[2689,"An agency building a client's webinar uses **Create with AI** with **Balanced Approach** to draft offers, edits them into the client's voice, and maps **Clicked offer** to a tag in the client's CRM."],[1883,"An agency building evergreen funnels for several clients uses AI Simulation to set up each room quickly, then switches individual webinars to Manual Simulation once a client wants a fixed, approved script."],[4911,"An agency builds a client's landing page in the client's site builder and pastes the widget in, so the page stays on the client's domain and in the client's design."],[4212,"An agency builds automated webinars from videos its clients keep on Vimeo or Wistia. Importing the links leaves the files where the clients manage them, and the platform badge in the Library shows which videos depend on an outside host."],[5076,"An agency builds registration pages for several clients. It works from the **Agency** or **Marketing** templates, applies each client's fonts and background in **Settings**, and pastes the client's pixel into **Tracking Code** so the client's ad account sees the traffic."],[2843,"An agency keeps a webinar it has refined over several events and clones it as the starting point for each new one, unticking anything that should be set up fresh."],[3904,"An agency managing webinars that play videos from a client's YouTube channel checks the badges on the **Videos** tab before the client tidies up their channel, because removing a YouTube video breaks the webinar that plays it."],[520,"An agency often sends for a client whose domain it doesn't control, so the DNS work may sit with the client's own team. Raise it early, because DNS changes can take a few hours to propagate. A separate client workspace keeps that client's webinars, registrants and settings apart from yours."],[1408,"An agency running webinars for a client filters by **Webinar**, exports the attendees with their **Lead score** and **Offer clicked** columns, and hands the file to the client's sales team, who don't use EasyWebinar."],[4782,"An agency running webinars for clients sets the consent wording to each client's own policy and exports the filtered registrant list for the client after each session."],[4354,"An agency running webinars for several clients sets each webinar's **Sender name** to the client's company and the **Reply-to email** to the client's own address. Because the settings are held per webinar, each client's registrants hear from the client, not the agency."],[5217,"An agency runs a webinar for a client whose founder is the speaker. The agency sends the founder the **Co-presenter Join Link** and its own team member the **Moderator Join Link for Live room**. Neither needs a login to the agency's account, and the founder waits backstage until the host brings them on."],[5564,"An agency runs webinars for clients, with the client's own speaker presenting. The account manager sends the speaker the **Co-presenter Join Link** from **Share**, so the speaker doesn't need an EasyWebinar login, and adds a colleague as moderator under **Team & Moderators**."],[2189,"An agency that already handles conversations in an external chat tool runs a client's evergreen webinar with **Third party** chat, so messages land in the tool its team watches every day."],[101,"An agency webinar team gives its staff **User** logins on the agency account. Each client gets a client workspace, so that client's webinars, registrants and settings stay apart from everyone else's."],[1982,"An automated webinar has no presenter to announce the deadline at the right moment. Since each session plays without the coach, its deadline has to keep time without the coach as well. The timer therefore belongs on the offer, which every session's audience sees."],[4710,"An EasyWebinar registration form starts with two required fields, **Full name** and **Email**. Select the form block in the page builder and you can add a **Phone** field, add your own custom fields, turn on a **GDPR Compliance** consent checkbox, and turn on **UTM Params** so each registration records the campaign that brought it in. What you collect then shows up in the **People** view and can be mapped into a connected CRM."],[3441,"An evergreen schedule can offer just-in-time sessions, On Demand viewing and a recurring daily or weekly schedule. All three can run at once, and the registrant picks."],[4586,"An evergreen webinar can offer three ways to watch. On Demand is the one with no wait at all: the registrant doesn't pick a session time, they start the recording when they're ready."],[4487,"An evergreen webinar generates a long run of sessions from one schedule, and can add just-in-time sessions that start shortly after someone registers. Reminders are timed relative to the webinar rather than to a clock, which is why they work for recurring and evergreen schedules without being set session by session."],[3359,"An evergreen webinar is one of EasyWebinar's three types. Live webinars are presented in real time; simulive webinars broadcast a recording at times you set, with everyone watching together. Evergreen also plays a recording, but the schedule repeats and each registrant chooses their own time."],[3296,"An evergreen webinar plays a recording on a schedule that keeps running, instead of on one fixed date. A visitor to the registration page picks a session that suits them, and the recording plays for them at that time. You set up the schedule once, with any combination of just-in-time sessions, On Demand viewing and recurring daily or weekly times, and EasyWebinar generates the sessions from it."],[942,"An evergreen webinar runs continuously, so every new visitor can watch soon after they find you. Its schedule has three options, and you can use any combination:"],[2686,"An evergreen webinar teaches one core idea and closes with an enrolment offer pointing at the course checkout on the creator's course platform. A countdown marks a bonus window, and a **Webinar Replica** replay keeps the offer working for people who watch later."],[2025,"An evergreen webinar teaches the core idea of a course and ends with an enrolment offer. A countdown on the offer marks the window for an enrolment bonus. Because the webinar is automated, the timer carries the deadline for every session without the creator being there."],[696,"An integration links one webinar to a CRM or email tool you already use. It has two halves. **Event mappings** apply a tag when someone does something, such as attending or missing the session. **Custom fields** copy webinar details, such as the title and each person's join link, into fields on the contact."],[2586,"An offer in EasyWebinar is a card with a headline and a button that you show to attendees during a webinar. The button sends people wherever the next step is: a checkout, a booking page or a sales page. You build offers on the webinar's **CTAs** tab, with an optional price and an optional countdown, then push them to the room when you make your pitch. Afterwards, Analytics shows how many people saw, clicked and bought each offer, and what it earned."],[2605,"An offer is not the same as charging for the webinar itself. Paid registration sells access to the session before it starts; an offer sells something separate, during or after it. EasyWebinar reports the two separately."],[2606,"An offer turns your pitch into something attendees can act on. It puts one button on screen at the moment you ask, pointing at the exact next step. Nobody has to remember a web address or hunt for it later."],[517,"An office admin or owner usually does this alone, with a first send already in the diary. Validating a single business address is the quick way to get going. Move to a domain once sending becomes regular, and ask whoever manages your website to help with the DNS."],[2188,"An onboarding session runs as a simulive webinar. The recording does the teaching, and the customer success team sits in the chat answering setup questions live while it plays."],[1942,"Analytics"],[2088,"Analytics"],[2411,"Analytics"],[4412,"Analytics"],[4688,"Analytics"],[4972,"Analytics"],[5452,"Analytics"],[1620,"Analytics and People"],[3274,"Analytics and People"],[3962,"Analytics and People"],[4272,"Analytics and People"],[1500,"Analytics counts how far people get through a webinar, from the page they land on to the offer at the end. Each stage of the funnel is controlled by its own part of your setup. So a weak number tells you which part to work on: the page, the reminders, the talk, the offer or the checkout."],[838,"and **Continue to Green Room** stays greyed out."],[3783,"Another of your events is live. EasyWebinar allows one live event at a time."],[2405,"Answer questions in the live room"],[4590,"Any combination can be switched on, and the registration page lists each enabled option as a choice."],[5206,"Anyone holding the **Co-presenter Join Link** or the *