e to Video Selection**, where you pick the recording the sessions will play."],[4136,"Simulive and evergreen webinars play a recording, so before you can run one you need a video in your library. EasyWebinar gives you two ways to get it there. **Local import** uploads the file and EasyWebinar hosts it for you, so you don't need a separate video host. **Import from URL** plays a video that already lives on **YouTube**, **Vimeo**, **Wistia** or at a direct **MP4** link. The route you pick decides whether the video uses your storage and whether you need to tell EasyWebinar how long it runs."],[3868,"Simulive and evergreen webinars play a video from your library:"],[3181,"Simulive is for a presentation you give again and again, or a session at an hour you'd rather not be awake for. You record the talk once. EasyWebinar then broadcasts it at the times you choose, like a scheduled programme."],[3207,"Simulive set-up, from each platform's public documentation:"],[1773,"Simulive webinars"],[2081,"Simulive: answer while the recording plays"],[1561,"Single-webinar reporting, from each platform's public help docs:"],[2292,"Skip polls and you present without knowing who is listening or what they need. Attention drifts through the long sections, and nobody is asked to do anything until the offer. After the session, attendance is all you have to show who took part."],[169,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[358,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[1625,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[1776,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[3280,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[4120,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[4978,"Small businesses and solo operators"],[4809,"SMS and WhatsApp (both Beta) go to the phone number from the registration form. Add the **Phone** field first."],[4773,"SMS and WhatsApp notifications, both in Beta, are set on the **Notifications** tab and go to the phone number from your registration form. Without the **Phone** field on the form, there's nobody to message. Reminder emails cover the email side."],[4267,"SMS and WhatsApp welcome messages (Beta)"],[1154,"So what should a thank-you email after a webinar say? Say thanks once, name the single idea people came for, give the replay and offer one next step. Leave out the full summary, because they were there. Only keep the reply line if the reply-to address is one somebody actually reads."],[968,"Some course creators charge for the webinar itself: a paid masterclass, a workshop, or a paid preview of the course. Choose **Paid** when you create the webinar, set the price in US dollars, and attendees pay through Stripe as they register. Turn on the **Live + Replay bundle** to sell the recording too."],[2319,"Some practical habits:"],[3466,"Someone registers on your registration page, paying first if the webinar is paid."],[1652,"Someone who arrives at your page at 2:07 can be watching by 2:15, instead of waiting until tomorrow. The recording is the same one your other evergreen sessions play; only the start time changes."],[416,"SPF lets a domain owner name the hosts allowed to use its domain, and a receiving system can check that permission. Think of it as a published guest list. A domain can't have several competing SPF records, so a new sending service joins your existing record rather than getting a second one."],[333,"SPF: who may send for your domain"],[572,"Start here"],[472,"Start here for every send. The notice above the list says what must be verified, and the Sending health label sits beside the default service."],[2140,"Stay on **Chat**, the first of the four sections (**Chat**, **Q&A**, **AI Moderation** and **Chat Simulation**)."],[4463,"Stay on the **Emails** channel and find the **Webinar Reminders** group."],[2733,"Step 1: Open the clone dialog"],[2886,"Step 1: Open the create dialog"],[3063,"Step 1: Open the create dialog"],[5431,"Step 1: Read the four stats"],[3247,"Step 1: Start a new webinar"],[5432,"Step 2: Check or change the schedule"],[2734,"Step 2: Choose the type of the copy"],[3248,"Step 2: Name it and choose Evergreen"],[3064,"Step 2: Name it and choose Simulive"],[2887,"Step 2: Name your webinar and choose its type"],[3249,"Step 3: Build the schedule"],[5434,"Step 3: Manage individual sessions"],[2735,"Step 3: Name the copy"],[3065,"Step 3: Set the schedule"],[2888,"Step 3: Set your schedule"],[2894,"Step 4: Choose free or paid"],[3066,"Step 4: Choose free or paid"],[3255,"Step 4: Choose free or paid"],[2736,"Step 4: Choose what to copy"],[5435,"Step 4: Set free or paid registration"],[5436,"Step 5: Add presenters and moderators"],[2896,"Step 5: Choose a registration template"],[3067,"Step 5: Choose the recording"],[3256,"Step 5: Choose the video"],[2737,"Step 5: Create the copy"],[3257,"Step 6: Choose a template"],[2738,"Step 6: Finish a copy that changed type"],[3070,"Step 6: Pick a registration template"],[5438,"Step 6: Share the webinar"],[5439,"Step 7: Enter the room"],[834,"Still stuck?"],[3813,"Storage and your plan"],[4115,"Storage and your plan"],[880,"Stream to your existing audience"],[619,"Streaming"],[5408,"Stripe. Connect your Stripe account on the pricing step or from **Integrations → Payment**."],[1037,"Style the live room: backgrounds, overlays and layout so the room matches your course brand."],[3954,"Style the page in the builder"],[598,"Supported integrations"],[1069,"Swap in your own next step"],[3173,"Swapping the video leaves your schedule, pages and registrations as they were. That makes it safe to replace a presentation with an improved cut while sessions are already booked."],[2650,"Switch off the toggle on the offer's row in the **CTAs** list, or"],[2648,"Switch on **Countdown timer** to put a countdown on the offer card. It's a single toggle, set on each offer separately. Use it when the offer really does close or change at a set time, and say in the sub headline what ends."],[1987,"Switch on **Countdown timer**."],[4079,"Switch on **Enable replay page** and send the **Replay Page Link**. It's also in the **Share** dialog as **Attendee Replay link**."],[4017,"Switch on **Enable replay page**. The page then reads *Replay page is live, anyone with the link can watch*."],[1672,"Switch on **Just-in-time Registration**."],[4611,"Switch on **On Demand**."],[3325,"Switch on as many of the three viewing options as you like. Each one you enable shows on the registration page as a choice."],[1535,"Switch to **People** to see who registered and who attended. For a full list across all your webinars, with columns such as watch percentage, offer clicked and lead score, plus filters and export, use People in the sidebar."],[1536,"Switch to **Revenue**."],[822,"Symptom"],[1396,"Tag the links in your ads, emails and posts as you normally would."],[4752,"Tag the links you share as you normally would, in ads, emails and posts."],[1980,"Take a typical case. A business coach runs an evergreen webinar that plays twice a day and ends on an offer for a twelve-week coaching programme. Anyone who enrols before their session ends gets a bonus strategy call. The coach is rarely watching, and every session has a new audience."],[4157,"Take a typical case. A cooking instructor films a 45-minute sourdough class in her home kitchen. She wants it to run as an evergreen webinar for the 2,000 people on her newsletter, ending with a link to her paid six-week course. The class is part of what she sells, so she doesn't want it sitting free on a public channel."],[2455,"Take a typical case. A customer success team at a software company runs a live release briefing for its customers every quarter. Around 300 account admins attend, and many arrive with questions about their own setup. One product manager presents, while two success managers support the session."],[4876,"Take a typical case. A dental practice group with six clinics runs a free monthly live webinar on clear aligners for prospective patients. Its marketing coordinator works alone, and the group's aligner treatment page already draws steady visitors. The goal is for those readers to sign up without leaving that page."],[410,"Take a typical case. A family-run garden centre with two branches has 1,800 leads from its spring planting webinars. The office admin is asked to send the first broadcast on Monday, for an offer closing on Friday. She opens the CRM, writes the email, and learns that nothing can go out yet."],[2134,"Take a typical case. A financial planning firm runs a monthly live webinar for people within a few years of retirement. Around 120 people attend, and many want to ask about their own pension or savings. One planner presents, and a colleague supports the session."],[2828,"Take a typical case. A fitness coach who works alone runs a free live kick-off for her 30-day kettlebell challenge each month. About 120 people register, and each kick-off ends with an offer to join her paid coaching group."],[4734,"Take a typical case. A five-person cybersecurity consultancy runs a quarterly live briefing on ransomware readiness for IT managers at mid-sized firms. It wants each registrant's role and company size, so its two consultants follow up with the right people. It also needs consent before any marketing email goes out."],[4318,"Take a typical case. A mortgage broker with a two-person office runs a free monthly webinar for first-time buyers. Around 60 people register from local ads and estate agent referrals. The broker's aim is a follow-up call with anyone ready to apply."],[1818,"Take a typical case. A novelist is launching a monthly online book club for readers of literary fiction. She records a 30-minute introduction to the club and runs it as an evergreen webinar, linked from her newsletter. She expects around 40 viewers a day, spread across several sessions."],[4011,"Take a typical case. A personal-finance educator runs a free live class on clearing credit card debt, on the first Thursday of each month at 19:30. About 250 people register, many of them parents and shift workers. The class ends with an offer to join an eight-week budgeting programme."],[5188,"Take a typical case. A podcast network with six shows hosts a monthly live webinar where a guest from one show gives a talk. The network's producer runs the session and a show host interviews the guest. Promotion goes out on every show's feed and newsletter, and the guest has never used the network's webinar account."],[3009,"Take a typical case. A real-estate training company with three instructors runs a live exam review for about 80 trainee agents. It meets on Tuesday and Thursday evenings for the eight weeks before each licensing exam, and it pauses on public holidays."],[5552,"Take a typical case. A regional wildlife charity has a fundraising team of three and around 900 monthly donors. Each month it runs a live 40-minute briefing for donors, with a guest speaker from its field team. The goal is to keep monthly givers engaged, so fewer cancel their gift."],[89,"Take a typical case. A six-person digital marketing agency runs monthly lead-generation webinars for four accounting firms. Two producers build the registration pages, an account manager hosts, and a junior marketer moderates the chat. Each firm expects its own leads and settings to stay private."],[2294,"Take a typical case. A small marketing agency runs a monthly live workshop on local search for owners of small businesses. About 150 people register, and the agency wants to know which of them are worth a call afterwards. The two partners present, and neither has time to watch the chat closely."],[1665,"Take a typical case. A small skincare brand sells online with a team of four, and most new visitors arrive from social ads. It has made a 20-minute recorded webinar that walks through a morning and evening routine. The goal is to turn ad clicks into first orders."],[3188,"Take a typical case. A small Spanish language school with five teachers offers a free taster session for adult beginners before each term. Around 40 people join each sitting, held on Saturday mornings and Wednesday evenings for the six weeks before enrolment closes."],[2613,"Take a typical case. A software-training company with four instructors runs a monthly live webinar on passing a cloud architect exam. About 250 IT professionals attend. The session ends by selling a certification bundle of three practice courses and an exam voucher."],[207,"Take a typical case. A solo marketing consultant who helps local accountancy firms win clients plans her first webinar. It's a 40-minute live session on getting referrals through LinkedIn, two weeks away. She has about 400 contacts and wants to end with an invitation to her three-month advisory programme."],[3738,"Take a typical case. A university admissions office runs a live open evening each term for prospective postgraduate students. Two admissions officers present, a current student joins for the last twenty minutes, and around 200 applicants from several countries register."],[1124,"Take a typical case. A watercolour teacher sells a six-week online course, and this morning she ran a free live class on mixing skin tones. She closed with an offer, and enrolment stays open until Friday. It's now lunchtime, and she still has to write her follow-ups."],[5015,"Take a typical case. A wedding photography educator runs a free live workshop on posing couples each month, with one assistant helping her. Each workshop feeds a small paid mentoring group, and her goal is about 150 signups from Instagram and her email list."],[4457,"Take a typical case. A yoga teacher-training school runs a free evergreen info session for people thinking about its 200-hour course. The session plays several times a week, and people register from the school's Instagram. Most sign up on their phones, often days before the session they pick."],[3533,"Take a typical case. An employment law firm runs a quarterly live panel for HR directors on new workplace rules. Two partners host, and two guest speakers join from outside the firm. Around 180 people attend, and the session is recorded for clients who miss it."],[4604,"Take a typical case. An HR software vendor sells leave and payroll tools to companies with 50 to 200 staff. Its marketing team of three records a 15-minute explainer on a change to holiday pay rules and links it from a monthly newsletter. The aim is to get HR managers to book a product demo afterwards."],[1356,"Take a typical case. An independent insurance agency with a six-person sales team runs a fortnightly live webinar on choosing life cover for young families. About 180 people register through social ads and a partner newsletter. Each session ends with an offer to book a quote call."],[3890,"Take a typical case. The operations team at a franchise with 40 bakery outlets runs a live training session each month for new store managers. It covers opening checks, food safety and the till system. Managers who join later need the same training, usually in their first week."],[917,"Take a typical example. A course creator opens enrolment with a live webinar in launch week, presenting to an email list and answering questions in the chat. The launch closes, but new subscribers keep arriving. The creator wants that same pitch to enrol them without presenting it every week."],[5139,"Team & Moderators"],[761,"Team and account"],[106,"Team and client access, from each platform's public documentation:"],[1,"Team members and roles"],[3492,"Team members and roles"],[751,"Team members and roles: give the colleagues who manage your integrations their own login."],[854,"Team members and roles: who joins as a co-host or moderator, and how they get in."],[5437,"Team members are a different thing"],[78,"Team members are one of three ways to bring people in:"],[82,"Team members let colleagues work in your account under their own names. Each person signs in separately, so nobody passes a password around. The role you pick decides whether they can reach billing and settings."],[10,"Team members, presenters and moderators"],[5254,"Team members: logins for colleagues, as opposed to join links for guests."],[5602,"Team members: when to give someone a login instead of a join link."],[624,"Teams running several webinars"],[4875,"Teams that already run websites and blogs want registration to sit inside the content that brings in traffic. It matters most to whoever owns the website, at the point a webinar is announced. That's when a form that drops into an existing page saves building a new one."],[2827,"Teams that run webinars as a programme reuse what works. Copying a proven webinar, or moving it from live to automated, without a rebuild is what makes that practical."],[1612,"Test a just-in-time session"],[4408,"Text and WhatsApp reminders (Beta)"],[4684,"Text reminders (Beta)"],[1271,"Thank people once, name the one idea they came for, share the replay link and offer a single next step. Keep it short, because attendees don't need a summary of a session they just watched."],[5136,"Thank You Page Link"],[1058,"Thank-you email after a webinar"],[1348,"That changes what you can do after a session. Instead of knowing that a large share of registrants missed the webinar, you can see who they are, narrow the list to one webinar, and export it for a replay email. Instead of knowing that some people clicked your offer, you can find the ones who watched most of the session, clicked, and still haven't bought."],[3380,"The **AI Moderator** answers attendee questions from documents you upload and keeps the chat civil. It's available from Pro onward. It's aimed at large webinars and automated ones running without you."],[1704,"The **AI Moderator**, available from Pro onward, answers attendee questions from documents you upload and keeps the chat civil. On an automated webinar running at 3am, it can answer a question that would otherwise wait for someone on your team."],[1400,"The **Analytics** tab on a single webinar shows the funnel and offer numbers; People shows the individuals across all webinars. Use Analytics to find where a webinar is leaking, then People to find who is in that gap. See Webinar analytics."],[4906,"The **Clone Webinar** dialog lists **Embed widgets** under **Webinar assets**, alongside registration pages, the webinar schedule and replay settings. Leave it ticked to copy the widget into the new webinar."],[2598,"The **CTAs** tab holds two kinds of call to action. A poll asks your audience something. An offer asks them to do something: buy, book, enrol, download."],[683,"The **Custom fields** tab sends webinar details into your CRM's own fields: \"Choose which of your CRM's custom fields should receive each piece of webinar data.\""],[3574,"The **Design** panel sets the background, a frame overlay and the stage layout. Layout matters most when several presenters are on stage or someone is sharing a screen."],[2175,"The **EW Contact Box** is a built-in question and contact form for attendees, and it works even when chat is off. **No Chat** plus a pinned contact box suits a presentation you don't want interrupted but still want questions from."],[3863,"The **Files** tab is also where the AI Moderator's documents live. The AI Moderator, available from Pro onward, answers attendee questions from documents you give it, reading **PDF, DOCX or PPTX** files up to **25MB** each, and it retrains every time you add a source."],[1497,"The **Overview** view is built around a funnel. People arrive at the registration page, some register, some of those attend live or watch the replay, and some never show up. Below the funnel sit the numbers for what happened during the session: chat, questions, and how your offers performed."],[1498,"The **People** view lists who registered and who attended. The **Revenue** view breaks down what the webinar earned, separating paid registrations from offers sold in the room."],[1242,"The **People** view shows the individuals behind those numbers. Filter by **Behavior → no-show** for one webinar to see who still needs the replay. Filter by high watch time plus offer clicked to find people who were interested and didn't buy, then check their **Replay watch percentage** before you write to them again."],[710,"The **Phone Number** field only has something to send if your registration form collected it. Select the form block in the page builder and add the **Phone** field; custom registration fields covers the other fields you can add."],[1403,"The **Phone** column fills only if your form collects it. Add the **Phone** field, or your own fields, on the registration form block. See Custom fields."],[3199,"The **Plan** tab, under **Settings** → **Manage Plan**, counts people watching simulive and evergreen sessions on its **Automated attendees** meter, separately from **Live attendees**."],[3153,"The **Recordings** filter is where earlier webinars show up. If you ran a session live with recording on, its recording can become the content of this simulive webinar."],[4350,"The **Registered** metric in a webinar's Analytics counts unique sign-ups, which is the number of people your confirmation should have reached. The **People** view lists each registrant with a **Registered on** column. See Webinar analytics and People."],[4293,"The **Registration Confirmation** email is the first message a new registrant gets from your webinar. EasyWebinar sends it immediately after someone registers, and it carries their attendee join link, which makes it the email that actually gets people into the session. It sits on the webinar's **Notifications** tab, and the **Email Settings** button on the same screen decides whose name your emails show, where replies go, and whether an admin address receives a copy of each registration."],[5068,"The **Registration Page Link** in the Share dialog is the published page's address, and the **Registration Page** button on the webinar header opens the live page."],[4855,"The **Registration Widget** lets people register for your webinar without leaving your own website. On the webinar's **Content** tab you copy its **Embed Code** and paste it into your page wherever the form should appear: a landing page, a blog post, a sidebar. The same snippet is in the **Share** dialog, and **View & Customize** lets you change how the widget looks before you copy it."],[5067,"The **Registration Widget** on the Content tab gives you embed code to put the form on your own page instead of sending people to the EasyWebinar page."],[4627,"The **Replay** tab controls a separate page for people who missed a session, with availability options of **Always Available**, **Expire after days** or **Specific date**, and an **Experience** of **Webinar Replica** or **Custom Build**. Those settings belong to the replay page, not to On Demand."],[3997,"The **Replay** tab holds everything about the page people use to catch up on a past session. It has five parts:"],[2676,"The **Revenue** view splits earnings under **Revenue by Source**, separating paid registrations from offers sold in the room. Offer sales show under **Paid Offer**."],[5513,"The **Sessions** panel lists every sitting of the webinar under its own tab:"],[3572,"The **Share** dialog on the webinar holds seven links, including the presenter and moderator ones above. **Shorten URL's** gives you shorter versions. See Sharing links."],[714,"The **Social media** category on the Integrations tab is separate from CRMs. To broadcast a webinar to YouTube or LinkedIn while it runs, use EasyCast, under **Integrations** in the sidebar."],[5209,"The **Team & Moderators** panel on the Overview is the other way to bring people into a webinar: **Add presenter** adds someone who appears on camera and presents, and **Invite** sends an invitation to join the webinar team. A join link suits a guest on one session; adding someone to your account team suits people who work in EasyWebinar regularly."],[4344,"The **Thank you Page** is one of the five pages on the **Content** tab and appears straight after registering. On a paid webinar, it shows the attendee's **Webinar join link** with a **Join Room** button, and the session **Date** with **Add to Calendar** buttons for Google, Outlook and Apple. The page and the email cover the same moment from two sides: one in the browser, one in the inbox."],[3172,"The **Webinar Video** panel on the **Content** tab shows the selected recording and its length. **Preview** plays it back, and **Change Video** swaps it for another."],[1140,"The 2008 study's guidance is to start with what matters most to the reader. It warns that email opening with marketing risks being deleted before people reach what they need. A person who registered came for the session, so the replay link sits above the offer in these templates."],[238,"The AI drafts a full sequence:"],[2530,"The AI Moderator is available from Pro onward. In **Suggest Answer** mode it drafts answers for you to approve; in **Answer directly** mode it answers without approval."],[2493,"The AI Moderator, available from Pro onward, has a **Q&A Moderation** screen. With **Suggest Answer** it drafts an answer for you to approve before attendees see it; with **Answer directly** it answers without host approval. It doesn't replace a human moderator on a live webinar."],[3900,"The AI Moderator's knowledge base documents live on the **Files** tab. Because the moderator retrains with each new source, give it a moment after an upload before you test it."],[5059,"The AI then writes your registration page, thank-you page and waiting area, using the script as source material: headline, what you'll learn, instructor bio and the rest. The pages open in an editor with **Edit**, **Preview**, **SEO** and **Scripts** tabs, and a desktop and mobile view. Treat the output as a strong first draft; afterwards, pages open in the normal builder."],[237,"The AI writes your registration page, thank-you page and waiting area, using the script as source material: headline, what attendees will learn, an instructor bio and the rest. The pages open in an editor with **Edit**, **Preview**, **SEO** and **Scripts** tabs and a desktop and mobile view. Treat them as a strong first draft. Click **Continue**."],[1496,"The Analytics tab is the per-webinar report. It answers two questions: how many people got through each stage, from landing on the registration page to watching, and where the others stopped."],[5357,"The attendee lands on a Stripe checkout page showing your business name and the webinar as the item. They can pay by card, or by Apple Pay or Link where available. Their email is carried over from the registration form, so they don't type it twice."],[1770,"The attendee list"],[707,"The briefing itself doesn't need to change. What changes is how much of each person's session reaches the CRM, and how fast. An attendee who stayed to the end is a different lead from one who left after ten minutes, and the CRM should know that."],[5554,"The briefing itself rarely goes wrong. The risk sits in the handovers between people and tools before it starts. Fewer places to look means fewer chances to miss a detail."],[4957,"The builder controls"],[2956,"The builder opens on **Step 1 of 4: Build**."],[3683,"The button stays greyed out until the room can see a camera and a microphone. If it can't open your camera, you'll see **\"Not able to access selected media device\"**. Allow camera access in your browser and close any other app that's using the camera. Camera not detected walks through the fixes."],[3547,"The button stays greyed out until the room can see a camera and a microphone. If you see **\"Not able to access selected media device\"**, see Camera not detected."],[2615,"The buying decision hasn't changed, but the moment it happens matters more. Attendees decide while the instructor is still talking, so the next step needs to sit on screen right then. It also helps to count each step between seeing the bundle and paying for it."],[1189,"The CAN-SPAM Act requires a commercial email to carry a \"clear and conspicuous notice\" that people can decline further messages. Once someone asks, you have 10 business days to stop sending them commercial email. Put a visible unsubscribe line at the foot of every follow-up that pitches something."],[1769,"The Chat setting"],[4759,"The checkbox is a form option. What it asks for, and how you use the data afterwards, is yours to decide."],[443,"The choice usually comes down to what you already run. If your company has an SES, SendGrid or Mailgun account that someone manages, connect it. If you have no provider account, start from the default row and leave provider work for later. Whichever you select, the verification notice still applies, so choosing a service never replaces verifying your identity."],[4013,"The class itself doesn't need to change. The people who missed it need the same session, with the same offer, and a clear point where the chance ends. The replay should finish where the live class finished: on the next step."],[4311,"The confirmation does two jobs at once. It tells the registrant that signing up worked, and it hands them the join link they need later. EasyWebinar's guidance calls it the one email you shouldn't turn off. Every other step of getting someone into the room depends on that link."],[4343,"The confirmation starts the email sequence and **Webinar Reminders** carry it to the session. Reminders share the same **Email Settings**, so the sender a registrant learns from the confirmation is the one they see again before the webinar. See Reminder emails."],[4486,"The confirmation starts the sequence and carries the join link; reminders bring the registrant back to it. Both are on the same tab and share the same sender settings."],[1199,"The cost is time on the day you can least spare it, and the lists start to age the moment you export them. Someone who watches the replay this evening still gets tomorrow's \"you missed it\" email."],[4959,"The countdown block"],[2017,"The countdown is part of an offer, so everything about the offer still applies: its price, its button and where that button goes. Point the button at the checkout for exactly the offer the timer describes."],[1511,"The counts are built to avoid double counting. **Visitors** counts unique IPs on the landing page. **Total Attended** counts each person once and files them under **Live only**, **Replay only** or **Both**. How other platforms report on a single webinar is covered in How webinar analytics compares, below."],[1764,"The CSV format"],[2677,"The date range at the top of Analytics applies to everything on the page, so widen it if an offer looks emptier than expected."],[1059,"The day-after email for attendees"],[1063,"The day-after email for no-shows"],[4622,"The deciding question is whether the webinar is valued for its content or for the occasion."],[4621,"The default *Watch instant replay* works, but the word \"replay\" can suggest a recording of something people missed. If your page sells the webinar as fresh content, wording that says what happens next, such as starting the training now, can read more naturally. Whatever you choose, keep it short enough to fit a button."],[2998,"The default form collects **Full name** and **Email**, both required. Use **Add Custom Field** to collect more on the form."],[3374,"The demo is the same every week, so the presenter is not what makes each session work. Once it is recorded, sessions can repeat by themselves. The wait can then shrink to minutes for anyone who registers between Tuesdays."],[845,"The device check shows your camera preview and microphone, and **Continue to Green Room** becomes clickable. Nothing in the green room is broadcast; the header reads **Not Live** until you click **Go Live**."],[1758,"The EasyWebinar approach to chat simulation"],[2746,"The EasyWebinar approach to cloning"],[4258,"The EasyWebinar approach to confirmation emails"],[1931,"The EasyWebinar approach to countdown timers"],[147,"The EasyWebinar approach to Create with AI"],[613,"The EasyWebinar approach to CRM mapping"],[4677,"The EasyWebinar approach to custom fields"],[3266,"The EasyWebinar approach to evergreen webinars"],[1605,"The EasyWebinar approach to just-in-time"],[2073,"The EasyWebinar approach to live chat"],[2904,"The EasyWebinar approach to live webinar scheduling"],[2546,"The EasyWebinar approach to offers"],[4545,"The EasyWebinar approach to On Demand"],[5265,"The EasyWebinar approach to paid webinars"],[2237,"The EasyWebinar approach to polls"],[2401,"The EasyWebinar approach to Q&A"],[4825,"The EasyWebinar approach to registration embeds"],[4401,"The EasyWebinar approach to reminder emails"],[3949,"The EasyWebinar approach to replay pages"],[865,"The EasyWebinar approach to selling courses"],[5127,"The EasyWebinar approach to sharing links"],[3079,"The EasyWebinar approach to simulive webinars"],[18,"The EasyWebinar approach to team members"],[3483,"The EasyWebinar approach to the green room"],[3807,"The EasyWebinar approach to the Library"],[3635,"The EasyWebinar approach to the live room"],[5448,"The EasyWebinar approach to the Overview tab"],[1304,"The EasyWebinar approach to the People view"],[4953,"The EasyWebinar approach to the registration page builder"],[4105,"The EasyWebinar approach to video hosting"],[1451,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar analytics"],[331,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar CRM email sending"],[1047,"The EasyWebinar approach to webinar follow-up emails"],[4250,"The email EasyWebinar sends the moment someone registers, carrying their join link, and the Email Settings that control the sender name, reply-to address and admin copies."],[1206,"The emails live on each webinar's **Notifications** tab, and the replay settings live on its **Replay** tab. Set the replay first, because your emails depend on it."],[4346,"The emails step of **Create with AI** drafts a full email sequence, beginning with a welcome email on registration and followed by reminders and follow-ups. After the build, the emails sit on the **Notifications** tab like any others. See Create a webinar with AI."],[960,"The enrolment offer is a card with a headline and a button, shown in the room when you're ready."],[709,"The events table under Map events to tags lists all eleven triggers, including **Left early** and **Stayed on webinar at least**. How other platforms tag behaviour and send webinar data to a CRM is covered in How CRM mapping compares, below."],[601,"The events you can trigger on"],[4606,"The explainer has no live element that needs everyone together. Its value is the content, so the timing matters only to the reader. When a manager has fifteen spare minutes now, that is the moment to watch."],[1116,"The first two rows and the day-after rows follow the same timing as the follow-ups an AI-built webinar drafts. The rest are sensible starting points, so move them to fit your own deadline."],[262,"The flow ends on the new webinar's **Overview**. From here it works exactly like a webinar built by hand."],[4960,"The form block"],[5066,"The form block is where you collect more than name and email."],[4720,"The form block's settings add four things on top of that:"],[4774,"The form is one block on the registration page. Everything else on the page, from the template to the countdown and page-wide settings, is covered in Registration page builder."],[5044,"The form starts with **Full name** and **Email**, both required. Selecting the form block lets you add a **Phone** field and your own custom fields, turn on **GDPR Compliance** and **UTM Params**, and restyle the submit button. Each of these is covered under Custom fields, consent and UTM capture below."],[5559,"The four stats are a summary of what the **Analytics** tab shows in detail. A brand-new webinar shows zeros on both until registrations start."],[1205,"The full steps are in the next section."],[1678,"The full walkthrough, including pricing, video selection and templates, is in Create an evergreen webinar."],[277,"The generated sequence lives on the **Notifications** tab with every other email, where you can change subjects, content and timing. See Confirmation emails and Reminder emails."],[3474,"The green room and backstage"],[3516,"The green room is the private state of the EasyWebinar live room before a broadcast starts. You and your team can test cameras, microphones and screen sharing and arrange the stage, while the header reads **Not Live** and nothing reaches attendees. Backstage is the waiting area inside the room: co-presenters and moderators arrive there, off stage, and appear to the audience only when you move them on."],[3685,"The green room is the room as it will look, with nothing broadcast. Check your framing, test **Share**, and get your co-hosts in place."],[3526,"The green room separates getting ready from being seen. Live presenting has a few things that can go wrong in the first minute, such as a muted microphone or the wrong window shared. The green room lets you find them while nobody is watching."],[5190,"The guest doesn't need an account; they need a way into the room for one evening. Listeners don't need a different page; they need a link short enough for show notes. Newsletter subscribers the network already knows shouldn't have to fill in a form again."],[3706,"The header now shows **LIVE** with a timer of how long you've been broadcasting, the recording badge changes to **Recording**, and **Go Live** becomes **End Session**. If you're streaming with EasyCast, a platform icon appears for each destination. Click it to open the stream on that platform and see what viewers there are seeing."],[2642,"The headline and sub headline do the selling, so give each a job:"],[692,"The integration shows under **Connected apps** as **Ready to use**."],[412,"The job itself hasn't changed. Mail has to leave under a name that belongs to the business, and someone has to read the replies. That work only needs doing once if it lives where the broadcasts and workflows live. Every later send can then rely on it, so a busy admin doesn't repeat it."],[2830,"The kick-off is the same each month apart from the date. She also wants a version that runs without her, for people who find her between challenges. Both needs call for copying the setup rather than redoing it."],[1135,"The last row needs care. A fixed date can't be true for everyone when people watch an evergreen webinar on different days. Set the replay to **Expire after days**, then write \"in three days\" in your email rather than a date."],[1060,"The last-call email for attendees"],[1064,"The last-call email for no-shows"],[1067,"The left-early email"],[3085,"The Library"],[3788,"The Library"],[4112,"The Library"],[2749,"The Library and recordings"],[3878,"The Library is the account-wide store for everything you upload or record. Simulive and evergreen webinars get their content from it, finished live sessions end up in it, and your storage allowance is spent there."],[3883,"The Library is the shared store behind your webinars. It is where simulive and evergreen webinars get their content, and where finished live sessions end up. A video added once can be used by any webinar that needs it."],[2878,"The Library: where the recording of a live original waits to be picked."],[4090,"The Library: where the recordings your replay pages play are kept."],[1036,"The Library's recordings: where your live session's recording lands before you clone it as Evergreen."],[5180,"The links fall into three kinds. **Audience links** (registration, one-click registration, attendee join, attendee replay) are meant for the people watching. **Team links** (co-presenter and moderator) let someone into the room in a role, so they're private. The **Thank You Page Link** is where registrants arrive on their own, so you rarely need it."],[473,"The list is headed **Select Email service**, and it invites you to connect an SMTP provider to send emails from EasyCRM. **EasyCRM Mail** is already on the list, marked **Default**. To use your own provider, click **Add Service**, then **Configure** on the one you use, and enter its credentials. The section on built-in mail or your own SMTP provider, above, explains how to choose."],[22,"The live room"],[3810,"The live room"],[5140,"The live room"],[3730,"The live room is where a live webinar actually happens. Everything you set up beforehand leads to this hour. The room gives you a private place to prepare, a clear start, the tools to engage and sell, and a definite end."],[3724,"The live room is where you present a live webinar. You arrive in a green room where nothing is broadcast, then go live when you're set. The same room holds backstage, chat, polls, offers, questions and the recording controls."],[839,"The live room needs a working **camera and microphone** before it lets you into the green room. The button stays greyed out until the room can see both. You can turn them off once you are in, but you can't enter without them."],[3896,"The live room's header shows **Auto Recording Enabled** when recording is on, and the badge changes to **Recording** once you're live. Recording can be changed under **Settings → Recording Settings**, and recorded sessions land in **Library → Recordings**. See Run a live webinar."],[840,"The message appears when the room can't open the camera or microphone you selected."],[1843,"The mix of attendees decides whether the chat reads naturally. A room full of enthusiastic experts posting constantly looks staged. A few beginners asking basic questions, one sceptic, a couple of quiet observers on low engagement and some mid-level regulars reads like a natural conversation."],[4591,"The names overlap, so the two are easy to mix up."],[76,"The new member appears in the Team list with their role and joined date, and the seat count on the **Settings** overview goes up by one."],[474,"The notice on the screen sets out the requirement. You must either complete **Domain Configuration** for a dedicated domain, or validate a single business email address under **Email addresses**. Either route counts, and nothing sends until one of them is done. The **Domain Configuration** link in the notice and the **Domains** button both sit on this screen."],[3434,"The objects you work with"],[1973,"The offer countdown lives inside the **offer** itself, so it travels wherever that offer is shown. The registration-page countdown is a **block** on the page, so you style and position it like any other part of the page."],[2617,"The offer editor screenshot under Create an offer shows **Countdown timer**, **Offer Price** and the **Button** settings side by side. How other platforms handle offer countdowns and offer reporting is covered in How offers compare, below."],[2001,"The offer stays ready but does not appear until you switch it on."],[1066,"The offer-click email"],[1997,"The offer's **Title** is internal. Attendees never see it, so name it in a way that helps your team, for example \"Course enrolment, launch week\"."],[3049,"The one you set for the webinar. A live session runs at one fixed time for everyone, and the registration page shows that time with its timezone."],[5551,"The Overview matters most to teams running more than one webinar a month. Each webinar needs a quick status check and the right links for the right people. That need grows when guest speakers change from one session to the next."],[20,"The Overview tab"],[2908,"The Overview tab"],[5386,"The Overview tab has a **Convert to paid** button for free webinars. If you convert an existing free webinar to paid, its registration page keeps the free form, so people can still register without paying. Create a new webinar and choose **Paid** on the pricing step instead."],[5420,"The Overview tab has a **Convert to paid** button, but the registration page of a converted webinar keeps the free form. Create a new webinar and choose **Paid** on the pricing step instead."],[5543,"The Overview tab is the first of nine tabs on every webinar, and the one each webinar opens on. It summarises the webinar rather than configuring its content: numbers at the top, then schedule, sessions, pricing and team."],[5545,"The Overview tab is the home screen for one webinar. It answers everyday questions without opening any of the other eight tabs. Is anyone registering, when does it run, what does it cost, who is presenting, and which link do you send?"],[2879,"The Overview tab: checking the copy's schedule and its empty registrant count."],[3611,"The Overview tab: where **Enter Room** opens the green room for a session."],[4942,"The Overview tab: where **Registrants** confirms your test registration."],[5117,"The Overview tab: where the **Registration Page** button opens the live page."],[5250,"The Overview tab: where the **Share** button sits, next to **Registration Page**."],[1746,"The Overview tab: where the Live tab shows a just-in-time session on air."],[1592,"The Overview tab's live stats: four of these numbers at a glance, updating while the webinar runs."],[5017,"The page and the webinar describe the same event. So it makes sense for the page to read the schedule from the webinar, not from a copy someone keeps in step. When the date lives in one place, the page and the session can't disagree."],[4686,"The page builder"],[5100,"The page needs to be published. Open it in the builder and click **Publish**. Saving alone isn't enough."],[437,"The page setting needs the **C-Name** add-on plus a CNAME record, and it changes the public links people click. Treat them as two separate jobs, even if you use the same company domain for both."],[2806,"The panel at the bottom of the dialog repeats the new webinar's name and type before you commit. Read it once: it's the last chance to catch a wrong type, which can't be changed afterwards."],[3535,"The panel itself doesn't need to change. What matters is where the speakers wait and when the audience starts watching. If the checks happen out of view, the session can open on the first question instead of a sound test."],[574,"The parts of EasyWebinar"],[616,"The People view"],[2247,"The People view"],[2561,"The People view"],[4682,"The People view"],[4835,"The People view"],[1296,"The People view in EasyWebinar lists every registrant and attendee across all your webinars, with watch percentage, offer clicks and lead score, and lets you filter by behaviour or campaign and export the result."],[1355,"The People view matters to whoever owns the follow-up after a webinar, whether that's sales, marketing or a solo host. It matters most in the first day or two after a session, when per-person engagement data decides who gets a call first."],[4736,"The person signing up is the person answering the questions. So it makes sense to ask everything in one form, at the moment they register. Then the answers, the consent and the campaign all stay on the same record."],[209,"The pieces don't really need separate starting points. The script, the page, the emails, the polls and the offer all come from the same idea. If that idea is written down once, each piece can be drafted from it, and her time goes into editing instead."],[2326,"The poll stays on the webinar, ready to switch on, and nothing is deleted."],[2298,"The polls step lists seven goals, shown in the screenshot under Let AI write your polls, and returns five polls for 1 credit. Each poll comes with a short note on where it fits in the run of show. How other platforms handle polls is covered in How polls compare, below."],[2318,"The polls that get answered ask about the attendee, not about what they remember. A question about their situation, their plans or their biggest problem takes a second to answer and needs no right answer. A knowledge test feels like an exam, and people skip it."],[5065,"The price block is added only when **Paid** is chosen at creation. A free webinar converted to paid afterwards keeps the free form, so choose **Paid** at creation if you want to charge."],[5198,"The public link to your registration page, and the one you promote in ads, emails and posts. It only works once the registration page has been published in the page builder; saving alone isn't enough."],[2459,"The Q&A screenshot under Set up Q&A shows the three options side by side: **Public Q&A**, **Private Q&A** and **Disable**. After the briefing, the **Questions & Answers** panel in **Analytics** shows how many questions were asked and how many were answered. How other platforms handle attendee questions is covered in How Q&A compares, below."],[2296,"The questions the agency needs answered are the same every month. Only the audience changes. So the questions can be written once, with fixed answers, and kept with the webinar instead of being improvised on the day."],[4878,"The reader has already been convinced on the treatment page. So the form belongs there, next to the content that did the convincing, rather than one click away on another site."],[919,"The recording of the live session already holds the pitch that worked. What needs to change is who presses play: each new registrant, at a time that suits them, rather than the creator."],[4719,"The registration form is a block on the registration page, like the countdown or a heading. Its settings decide what a person is asked when they sign up. Out of the box it asks for a name and an email address, both required."],[4832,"The registration page"],[5271,"The registration page"],[5014,"The registration page decides how much of your promotion becomes an audience. It matters most to whoever pays for the traffic, in the weeks before a session. That's when a page that matches your brand and shows which campaign drove each signup earns its keep."],[5557,"The registration page is built on the **Content** tab and opened from the **Registration Page** button here. The **Registrants** stat counts people who complete its form."],[4902,"The registration page is still where your webinar's full pitch lives: the design, the countdown, the copy. Build and publish it in the page builder even if most registrations come through the widget, because the link and the page are what you'll use in email and on social."],[935,"The registration page is the link you promote, so it carries most of your signups. It opens in a drag-and-drop builder with your date, time and timezone already in place."],[4995,"The registration page is the page people see before they sign up, and the link you promote. EasyWebinar builds it in a drag-and-drop page builder that already carries your webinar's date, time and timezone, with a countdown and a name-and-email form in place. You choose a template, edit blocks on a canvas, check the mobile layout, and click **Publish** to make the page public. The same builder edits the webinar's other four pages."],[5005,"The registration page matters most because it carries your signups. It's built from **blocks**, such as the date and countdown block and the form block, plus whatever else the template includes. You select a block on the canvas to change it, add new blocks underneath, and set page-wide options such as fonts, background and tracking from the top bar."],[5240,"The registration page needs to be published. Open it in the page builder and click **Publish**."],[5354,"The registration page shows the price above the form."],[5007,"The registration page turns interest into a registrant. It says what the webinar is and when it runs, then asks for the details you need. After that, it hands each person on to the thank-you page."],[4459,"The reminders themselves don't change from one session to the next. What changes is the start time each one counts back from. A reminder set against the session, rather than the calendar, fits every session the schedule creates."],[877,"The replay"],[1062,"The replay email for no-shows"],[4045,"The replay link also appears in the webinar's **Share** dialog as **Attendee Replay link**, alongside the registration and attendee join links meant for your audience. See Sharing links."],[979,"The replay link is public while the replay page is on, so treat it as something anyone can share."],[4555,"The replay page"],[2663,"The replay page has two looks, set on the **Replay** tab:"],[4003,"The replay page is also the fifth of the five webinar pages on the **Content** tab, after the registration page, thank-you page, waiting area and webinar page. The Replay tab decides how the page behaves; the Content tab is where you can style it in the page builder."],[3382,"The replay page on the **Replay** tab is for people who missed a session that has already happened. It is separate from On Demand. See Replay pages."],[1437,"The result count is shown below the table."],[198,"The result is a normal EasyWebinar webinar. It has the same nine tabs and the same settings as one built by hand. The one visible difference is the **AI** badge on its card in **My Webinars**."],[3011,"The review is the same course with the same instructors every evening. What changes is only the calendar. So one webinar with a repeating schedule fits the course far better than sixteen separate ones, as long as the gaps can be marked."],[3774,"The room needs to see a working camera and microphone before it lets you in. See Camera not detected."],[1667,"The routine itself is not the problem. What matters is the time between the click and the first minute of the video. Ad traffic arrives all day and from more than one country, so any single fixed time is wrong for most visitors."],[851,"The same fixes apply on their computer. Co-hosts and moderators who get in land backstage, not on stage, so your audience doesn't see or hear them until you bring them on. A guest co-host or moderator joins with the Co-presenter Join Link or the Moderator Join Link from the Share dialog."],[342,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[1076,"The same job in EasyWebinar"],[1190,"The same section requires a valid physical postal address in commercial email. It also prohibits a subject line you know is likely to mislead a reader about the message. So \"Replay closes Friday\" is only safe to send if the replay really closes on Friday. Check each email for both, and add them if they aren't there."],[505,"The same split applies to text messages. A webinar's own SMS (Beta) is built on the same **Notifications** tab, and those messages go from your EasyWebinar number. That builder needs a phone number for each person, which comes from the Phone field on your registration form."],[1141,"The same study found that spreading one transaction across too many messages caused problems: people lost track and overlooked what mattered. Follow-ups aren't transactions, but the lesson carries over. That's the reason for stopping at about three emails per group, each with one next step."],[3318,"The schedule step is where an evergreen webinar differs from every other type. It offers three ways for people to watch, plus controls for when the schedule runs."],[3335,"The schedule step shows a message saying attendees will see times in their local time based on the selected timezone. On an evergreen webinar that applies to just-in-time sessions only. Daily and weekly sessions run at the times you set, in the webinar's timezone."],[3140,"The schedule step shows a message saying attendees will see times in their local time. For simulive sessions that isn't how they run: they play at the times you set, in the webinar's timezone. Only just-in-time sessions on evergreen webinars follow each attendee's local time."],[3133,"The schedule step works the same way it does for a live webinar."],[4322,"The screenshot under Choose who your emails come from shows the **Sender name**, **Reply-to email** and **Notify admin** settings in one dialog. How other platforms handle the sender, replies and admin copies is covered in How confirmation emails compare, below."],[211,"The screenshot under Describe your webinar shows the prompt box, **Type**, the five output chips and the credit balance on one screen. How other platforms use AI to build a webinar is covered in How Create with AI compares, below."],[1360,"The screenshot under Filter the list shows the six filter categories, from **Webinar** to **Device & Location**, with **Apply Filter** marked. How other platforms handle people lists, filters and exports is covered in How the People view compares, below."],[4461,"The screenshot under Find your reminders shows the **Webinar Reminders** group with reminders timed 15 minutes, 1 hour and 24 hours before the webinar. How other platforms set reminder timing and channels is covered in How reminder emails compare, below."],[5556,"The screenshot under Open the Overview tab numbers seven areas, from the nine tabs to **Team & Moderators**. How other platforms handle guest links, registration links and session history is covered in How the Overview tab compares, below."],[5192,"The screenshot under Open the Share dialog shows the co-presenter, moderator and one-click registration links in a single list. How other platforms handle presenter links, one-click registration and short links is covered in How sharing links compare, below."],[3537,"The screenshot under Read the room shows the **Not Live** status, the **Backstage** panel and **Go Live** in one view. How other platforms handle a pre-show room for speakers is covered in How the green room compares, below."],[4608,"The screenshot under Set it up shows the **Instant replay text** field, which reads *Watch instant replay* until you change it. How other platforms offer viewing straight after registration is covered in How On Demand compares, below."],[1669,"The screenshot under Set it up shows the **Just-in-time Registration** switch at the top of the schedule step, above **On Demand** and **Recurring Schedule**. How other platforms schedule sessions that start soon after registration is covered in How just-in-time compares, below."],[4015,"The screenshot under Set up a replay page shows **Availability** beside **Redirects** and **Experience** on one tab. How other platforms handle replay deadlines, offers and page design is covered in How replay pages compare, below."],[2138,"The screenshot under Set up chat shows both **Who can see messages** options, **Everyone** and **Hosts only**, beside the chat mode. How other platforms handle who sees attendee messages is covered in How live chat compares, below."],[1822,"The screenshot under Set up Manual Simulation shows the CSV upload beside **Preview Simulated Chat** and **Attendees on Event Page**. How other platforms handle simulated chat is covered in How chat simulation compares, below."],[269,"The script and pages are the expensive steps, which is another reason to get the prompt right before you generate. Each step shows your balance before you commit, and you can switch off any output chip to skip that part."],[933,"The script downloads as a PDF so you can present from it. A full build uses about 23 credits."],[235,"The script is organised into named sections, such as the hook and welcome and the problem framing, each with its own timing and speaker notes. Read it before you continue. Fixing the script now is much easier than fixing the pages and emails built from it. Click **Continue to landing pages**."],[1358,"The session doesn't need to change. What changes is the order of the calls. Someone who watched most of the webinar and clicked the offer should hear from an agent first. The ad budget should follow the channel that brought them."],[1126,"The session has already sorted her list. Some people came, some didn't, and some clicked the offer. She doesn't need one better email, just a short message for each of those groups, timed around Friday."],[3190,"The session itself barely changes from one sitting to the next. What differs is the questions people ask afterwards about levels and class times. So the presentation can be recorded once, and the teacher's time can move into the chat, while attendees still start together."],[394,"The setup rests on two separate decisions, and it helps to keep them apart. The **email service** is the system that carries your mail out of the CRM. The **sending identity** is the domain or address your mail comes from, verified so the CRM will send as you. Choosing a service doesn't verify your identity, and verifying your identity doesn't choose a service."],[5129,"The seven links"],[5119,"The seven links in an EasyWebinar webinar's Share dialog, from the registration page link you promote to the one-click registration, attendee, co-presenter, moderator and replay links, and who each one is for."],[4903,"The Share dialog groups every way to share a webinar: **Share Links**, **Registration Widget** and **Share on social media**."],[95,"The Share dialog holds a Co-presenter Join Link for a presenter and a **Moderator Join Link for Live room** for someone who handles chat and questions. Treat both as private. Anyone holding one can join your room in that role."],[5175,"The Share dialog is the single place to copy any link a webinar produces. Click **Share** on the webinar's header and the dialog opens with three groups:"],[5211,"The Share dialog's **Registration Widget** holds the same embed snippet as the **Content** tab. Use it to put the form on your own site instead of sharing a link."],[1287,"The Short Life of Online Sales Leads, Harvard Business Review, March 2011: the case for answering online enquiries quickly. One co-author ran a sales software company, and the full article is behind a paywall."],[3180,"The short version: simulive is a broadcast of a recording on a timetable. It sits between live, where you present every time, and evergreen, where the schedule runs continuously and each person picks their own time. For the evergreen setup, see Create an evergreen webinar."],[3854,"The small badge in the corner of each card shows where the video is hosted:"],[5499,"The stat cards across the top tell you, at a glance, whether people are signing up and turning up."],[93,"The Team screen pictured under Open the Team screen lists each member with a **Role** column reading **Owner**, **Admin** or **User**. How other platforms handle team logins, guest presenters and client accounts is covered in How team members compares, below."],[1174,"The templates above use a course because it's the clearest case, and our webinars for course creators page covers that audience in full. Everything in square brackets changes with the webinar. Keep the structure and change these parts."],[4266,"The thank-you page"],[5419,"The thank-you page shows their personal join link, a **Join Room** button, the session date and **Add to Calendar** buttons for Google, Outlook and Apple. They also get your confirmation email."],[3428,"The three webinar types"],[2950,"The three webinar types use the same pages, emails, polls, offers and analytics. Only the delivery differs."],[3427,"The three webinar types, what every webinar is made of, and how registration, the room, follow-up and analytics fit together."],[1214,"The To column is the one to check today. On this sample webinar the instant follow-up goes to Everyone, so change it before pasting in an attendee-only thank-you."],[2791,"The top of the dialog sets out what you're copying from and what you're creating."],[2136,"The topic doesn't have to change for people to speak up. What changes is who can read the message. When a question goes only to the firm, an attendee can ask it in plain words. The colleague can reply while the planner keeps presenting."],[5052,"The tracking, SEO, social and CSS areas also appear on the webinar's **Settings** tab, where they cover every page at once. Set them there to cover the whole webinar, or here for one page."],[5372,"The transaction list shows each buyer's name, email, session, registration date, amount and status. You can search it and filter by status."],[4035,"The two are easy to confuse, and they answer different needs."],[2673,"The two ratios point at different fixes:"],[3525,"The two together are what EasyWebinar's glossary calls the green room: a presenter backstage before the broadcast starts."],[3017,"The type is fixed once the webinar exists. To reuse a live webinar as simulive or evergreen, clone it and choose the new type on the copy."],[5561,"The type of a webinar can't be changed after it's created. To run the same webinar as a different type, clone it from its card on **My Webinars**. The copy starts with no registrations."],[952,"The type of a webinar is fixed, so the path from a live session to an evergreen funnel is cloning:"],[1289,"The UK's data protection legislation, GOV.UK: the data protection principles that apply to anyone using registrants' details."],[1243,"The useful measures for this job are the ones that show someone taking the next step: watching the replay, clicking the offer in the room, and buying. They have weak spots, so read them with care."],[3739,"The usual workaround is a general video meeting tool, set up by whoever is free that week. It may work, but the student often joins straight into the broadcast and tests their microphone in front of everyone. Questions can get lost in a busy chat, and the recording may end up in one person's account."],[3891,"The usual workaround is to download the recording from a meeting tool, upload it to a shared drive and email the link around. That can work, but copies end up in several places and nobody is quite sure which one is current. A link held in one person's account may stop working when they leave."],[411,"The usual workaround is to export the list and send from the shared office mailbox or a separate email tool. That may get one message out, but it tends to cost more with each send. Results sit apart from the lead records, replies scatter, and the next send starts from a fresh export."],[4158,"The usual workaround is to upload the class to a public video site as an unlisted video, then paste the link into a webinar tool. That can work, but unlisted links may be shared onwards, and the class now depends on a second account. If the video is removed there, the webinar may stop playing."],[3339,"The video you pick here is the recording every session plays, whichever way a registrant chooses to watch."],[3459,"The webinar header also has **Registration Page**, **Share** and **Enter Room**."],[2997,"The webinar is created and opens in the **registration page builder**, with your schedule applied and a live countdown to the session."],[2962,"The webinar name is empty. The builder won't move on until you enter one."],[3014,"The webinar opens straight into the registration page builder with your schedule applied. Edit the page, add form fields and publish it there. The registration embeds put the same form on your own site."],[5426,"The webinar Overview tab"],[3055,"The webinar Overview tab: change the schedule for the whole webinar, or act on a single session, after it exists."],[5421,"The webinar Overview tab: see registrants and revenue, and edit the price with the pencil."],[3785,"The webinar Overview tab: where **Enter Room** sits, and where past sessions are listed."],[819,"The webinar Overview tab: where one webinar's free or paid setting and live stats sit."],[506,"The webinar SMS builder (Beta) opens from a webinar's Notifications tab, so it isn't where you connect SMS Integration (Beta) for the CRM."],[2494,"The webinar's **Analytics** Overview includes a **Questions & Answers** panel showing how many questions were asked and how many you answered. The **Not Answered Rate** is a useful check after a busy session: it tells you how many questions you left behind."],[4776,"The webinar's **Analytics** tab counts visitors and registrations. The People view's **Source & UTM** filter shows which campaign produced attendees, not just registrations."],[4488,"The webinar's **Analytics** tab shows the funnel from **Visitors** to **Registered**, **Live Attended**, **Total Attended** and **Missed**. Two gaps tell you different things:"],[2662,"The webinar's **Settings** tab has an **End of Session** URL, which is empty by default. Point it at your offer or booking page so attendees aren't left sitting on a finished webinar. **Expired Session** does the same for anyone arriving at a link that has expired."],[91,"The webinars themselves don't need to change. What changes is who signs in, and where each client's work lives. Staff need their own names on their own logins, and each firm needs a space of its own."],[3013,"The weekly controls are easy to spot. With **Weekly** selected, the schedule step shows **Days of week**, **Session Time** with **Add Time**, and **Blackout Dates**. How this scheduling stacks up against other live webinar tools is covered in How live webinars compare, below."],[4869,"The widget is for keeping people on your site. If a visitor is already reading your page, the shortest path to a registration is a form right there, not a link to somewhere new."],[259,"Then describe what you're selling and where the button should send people, and pick an offer category: online course, coaching programme, membership, digital product or service package. Click **Generate Offers**."],[2658,"Then describe what you're selling and where the button should send people. Pick a category (online course, coaching programme, membership, digital product or service package) and click **Generate Offers**. You get complete offers, each with a title, headline, sub-headline, button text and link. Tick the offers you want to keep, then click **Save and continue**. The offers step costs 1 credit."],[2839,"There is no Delete for webinars. If you don't need a copy, choose **Archive** from its card menu and it moves to the **Archived** tab."],[5592,"There's a **Convert to paid** button, but the registration page of a converted webinar keeps the free form. Create a new webinar and choose **Paid** on the pricing step instead."],[2375,"There's no practical limit. Add as many as the session needs."],[5504,"These are headline numbers. For the full picture, open the **Analytics** tab: it adds **Visitors**, **Replay Watched** and **Missed**, and splits **Total Attended** into **Live only**, **Replay only** and **Both**."],[536,"These are the boundaries to plan around:"],[1181,"These are the errors that come from writing fast on the day of a session."],[428,"These are the providers' rules for senders, and they don't describe what EasyCRM does on your behalf. If your volume is nearing the bulk level, confirm with EasyWebinar how your setup meets each row before you scale up."],[2185,"These are typical patterns, showing how different teams set the chat up."],[715,"These are typical patterns, showing how different teams set their mappings up."],[100,"These are typical patterns, showing how different teams set up their logins."],[2685,"These are typical patterns, showing how offers fit different selling webinars."],[2840,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how cloning is used."],[1555,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how different teams read the same tab."],[1404,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how different teams use the same table."],[5216,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how each link fits a different job."],[4209,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how each route fits different kinds of webinar."],[3019,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how live scheduling fits different teams."],[4495,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how reminders fit different kinds of webinar."],[5074,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the builder fits different kinds of webinar."],[4351,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the confirmation fits different kinds of webinar."],[281,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the feature fits different users."],[2024,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the feature fits each kind of webinar."],[4778,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the form options fit different kinds of webinar."],[3575,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the green room and backstage fit different webinars."],[3901,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the Library fits different kinds of work."],[3748,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the live room fits different teams."],[4055,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the replay page fits different kinds of webinar."],[1708,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the setting is used."],[4631,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the setting is used."],[5562,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the tab fits different teams."],[3201,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the type fits different teams."],[3385,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the type is used."],[4907,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the widget fits different kinds of sites."],[2496,"These are typical patterns, to show how the setting changes with the kind of webinar."],[1879,"These are typical patterns, to show how the two modes fit different automated webinars."],[2348,"These are typical patterns, to show where polls fit in different kinds of webinars."],[5381,"These patterns show how paid registration fits different kinds of webinars."],[502,"These settings don't change your webinar reminder emails. They cover EasyCRM broadcasts and workflows, and a webinar's own messages are set on that webinar."],[420,"These standards are general, and they don't describe EasyCRM's screens. What's confirmed for EasyCRM is narrower: you complete **Domain Configuration** for a dedicated domain, it involves DNS work, and a verified domain gives better deliverability than a single address. Follow what that screen asks for, rather than copying records from another service or guide. This page names no specific EasyCRM records for that reason."],[5362,"They also receive your **Registration Confirmation** email, which carries their join link, and your reminders before the session."],[313,"They carry an **AI** badge on their card in **My Webinars**."],[2380,"They do when the replay page uses **Webinar Replica**, which copies the session's video, offers, polls and chat replay."],[3467,"They get confirmation and reminder emails from the Notifications tab."],[3468,"They join live, or watch the recording at their session time."],[806,"This allowance applies only if you use EasyCRM. It's your CRM or ours: keep HubSpot, Salesforce Pardot (Account Engagement), ActiveCampaign, Mailchimp or Kit (formerly ConvertKit), or use EasyCRM natively."],[846,"This appears on **Enter Room** when another of your webinars is already live, because only one webinar can be live at a time. Join that one from its Overview tab, or go live with the new one."],[453,"This is an illustrative calculation with sample figures, not an EasyWebinar output. It uses the garden centre from earlier and asks two planning questions. Does the month fit the **Wallet** allowance, and how close is the largest day to the level Gmail treats as bulk?"],[1248,"This is an illustrative calculation with sample figures, not an EasyWebinar output. Take the watercolour class from earlier, with 200 registrants, and the send plan from this page. Three emails go to each main group, and one goes to each behaviour group."],[3144,"This is the step that makes the webinar simulive. Whatever you pick here is what plays in every session."],[5187,"This matters most to teams that bring outside speakers onto their webinars and promote to lists they already own. It shows up in the week before a session, when links go out to speakers, moderators, customers and the wider audience at once."],[409,"This matters most to the admin or marketer who owns the first send, and to the owner whose domain carries the mail. It matters at the very start, before lists and campaigns exist. Domain work can need help from whoever holds your DNS, so leave time for it."],[504,"This screen is the other half of the picture: emails tied to one webinar, with their own sender settings, which your CRM sending setup leaves untouched."],[1169,"This sends on the action, so it can arrive while the person is still deciding. Write it to make sense whenever it lands, and don't assume they left without buying. The offer's button decides where they went, so link the same page here."],[403,"This setup answers one question: how do my CRM broadcasts and workflows go out from my own domain or address? It connects an email service and proves who you send as. You do it once, before you build anything."],[88,"This starts to matter when a second person joins the webinar work. That might be a producer building pages, a moderator in the chat, or a manager checking results. It matters most to agencies, whose own staff and each client's work need clear lines between them."],[1136,"Three emails per group is enough for most sessions. If you can only write two, keep the first and the last. The first delivers what people signed up for, and the last tells them what is about to close. For more on how the wider sequence fits together, read our guide to webinar retention and post-webinar follow-up."],[4518,"Three is a sensible default: a day before, an hour before, and fifteen minutes before. The last one does most of the work."],[4476,"Three is a sensible default. Webinars built with **Create with AI** arrive with exactly this pattern already drafted: **Reminder — 1d**, **Reminder — 1h** and **Reminder — 15m**, with merge tags already in place so dates, times and join links fill themselves in."],[1274,"Three per group is enough for most sessions: one right after, one a day later and a last call before your deadline. If nothing is closing, you can stop at two."],[3354,"Three things confirm the setup worked:"],[2835,"Ticking **Registration pages** and **Embed widgets** brings the original's pages and embed widgets across to the copy. Review them on the copy before promoting it, since the copy may now be a different type with a different schedule. See Registration page builder and Registration embeds."],[2889,"Timezone"],[1611,"Timezones: who sees which time"],[3777,"To **Library → Recordings**, named after the webinar with its duration and recording date."],[5519,"To act on one sitting without touching the rest, use the options menu on that session's row. To change every sitting at once, go back to **Scheduling Options**."],[5042,"To add a block, use the orange **+** below an existing block. The new one goes underneath."],[2626,"To add a new one, click **+** at the bottom of the offer list on the left."],[3871,"To change it later, open the webinar's **Content** tab and use the **Webinar Video** panel. **Preview** plays the current video and **Change Video** swaps it."],[2003,"To change it:"],[5510,"To change the date, time or recurrence, open the options menu on the **Scheduling Options** panel. A change made here applies to the whole webinar, not to one sitting."],[2173,"To check it, join a running session as an attendee, not as an admin. The widget sits under **Contact** in the right-hand rail, on its **Chat** tab. In the admin view the side panel shows your own host tools instead."],[941,"To keep people on your own site, copy the **Registration Widget** embed code and paste the form into a landing page or blog post."],[3570,"To leave without broadcasting, click **Exit**. For what happens once you're live, see Run a live webinar."],[3663,"To run a live webinar in EasyWebinar, you open the webinar room from the webinar's **Overview**, pass a quick camera and microphone check, and arrive in the green room, where nothing reaches your audience. When you're ready you click **Go Live**, present using 